The scientist's $T^{\prime}$-index starts at $1.0$, followed by a quick decline, and then stabilizing around $0.84$.

\subsection{Total Citations by Fibonacci-adjusted Contributions}
In the traditional citation counting all participating authors get the same amount of citations per publication. We propose \emph{total citations by Fibonacci-adjusted contributions},  $C^{\prime}$-index, where each author is credited according to their position in the byline of a publication as shown in Equation~\ref{eq_citation_credit}.
\begin{equation}
	\label{eq_citation_credit}
	C^{\prime} = \sum_{k=1}^{P} \frac{C_k}{F_{R(k)}}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $C^{\prime}$ is an author's total citations by adjusted contributions; $P$ is their number of participated publications; $C_{k}$ is the total citations of the $k$-th publication; $R(k)$ is the author's rank (position) in the $k$-th publication; and $F_{\boldsymbol{.}}$ is the Fibonacci number corresponding to the author's rank in the $k$-th publication.


