\subsection{$T^{\prime}$-index}

\begin{figure}
	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth,height=0.25\textheight]{plots/t_prime_index.pdf}
	\caption{$T^{\prime}$-index of authors by standardized publication rank.}
	\label{fig:t_index_participation_rank}
\end{figure}

The distribution of the $T’$-index of authors within our combined dataset is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:t_index_participation_rank}, where the x-axis represents the descending ranks of authors based on field-standardized, participation-based publication counts. The LOWESS curve reveals a slight upward trend in the $T^\prime$-index, starting at approximately 0.77 and stabilizing around 0.8 as the standardized participation rank increases. This suggests that most authors in our combined dataset have $T’$-index values exceeding the upper and lower benchmarks of 0.72 and 0.66, respectively, indicating that they predominantly take leading roles in their publications. The wide spread of data points below the benchmark reflects those authors who more frequently take on supporting roles, a pattern observed across all ranks. Notably, the data points situated below the lower benchmark and closer to higher ranks represent higher-prolific authors with $T^\prime$-index values below 0.66. Around 23.43\% of authors in our combined dataset fall below the lower benchmark value of 0.66. This suggests that these authors either predominantly collaborate in supporting roles or involve in potential cases of authorship abuse. We further analyzed the $T’$-index of authors in each field within our combined dataset and presented the figures in Appendix~\ref{t_index_by_publication_participation}, which drive the following discussions.

Authors in our Biology dataset have the lowest typical $T’$-index at 0.47. Electrical and Electronics Engineering, and Computer Science Engineering datasets exhibit typical $T’$-indexes of 0.71 and 0.76, respectively. Authors in the Psychology dataset has a typical $T’$-index of 0.78. Authors in the remaining field datasets, Sociology, Economics, Marketing, and Mathematics, have a typical $T’$-index of around 0.87.

In our Biology dataset, the typical $T’$-index indicates an upward trend starting at 0.4 and gradually increasing towards 0.6 for the lower-ranked authors. The trend remains below both the upper and lower benchmark values across the ranks. While some authors have a $T’$-index above the lower benchmark of 0.66, the majority of authors in this field fall below it. In our dataset, almost 93.5\% of authors in Biology are below the upper benchmark, and 86\% of authors are below the lower benchmark. This trend suggests that authors in the Biology dataset are more likely to engage in supporting roles rather than take on leading roles in a large number of papers.

In our Electrical and Electronics Engineering dataset, the typical $T’$-index displays a clear upward trend, starting at approximately 0.64. It passes the lower benchmark value around rank 200 and the upper benchmark value around rank 300, steadily rising to about 1.0 for the lower-ranked authors. In this dataset, roughly 50\% of the authors fall below the upper benchmark value, while 36\% are below the lower benchmark value. This trend signifies a gradual shift from supporting to leading roles as the rank decreases. The small group of higher-ranked authors with a $T’$-index below the benchmark may indicate that these contributors concentrate solely on supporting roles, despite their very high publication counts.

In our Computer Science and Engineering dataset, the typical $T’$-index displays a gradual upward trend, beginning at approximately 0.7, crossing the upper benchmark value around rank 150, and gradually increasing to about 0.9. In this dataset, almost 36\% of the authors are below the upper benchmark, and 22\% of the authors are below the lower benchmark. The small subset of higher-prolific authors near or below the benchmark indicates that these authors mostly take supporting roles compared to the others.

In our Psychology dataset, the typical $T’$-index shows a slow upward trend, starting at around 0.75 and gradually increasing to around 0.85 as rank decreases. Most authors in this field demonstrate a $T’$-index above both of the benchmark values, which may suggest stricter norms in authorship practices or that researchers predominantly take leading roles in their publications. The percentage of authors below the lower benchmark value is about 17\% in this dataset.

The typical $T’$-index for the Marketing, Economics, Sociology, and Mathematics datasets reveal broadly similar trends, with field-specific nuances. In all four field datasets, the typical $T’$-index values begin at relatively high levels, ranging from approximately 0.85 in Marketing to around 0.9 in Sociology and remain stable across most standardized ranks. This stability indicates that the majority of authors consistently take leading roles in their publications. Furthermore, a sharp rise in $T’$-index values is observed for lower-ranked authors in Marketing, Economics, and Sociology, whereas Mathematics exhibits a consistent trend without such a steep increase. The proportion of authors falling below the lower benchmark of 0.66 is relatively low among the collected authors of these fields, with Marketing showing the lowest proportion at 4.4\% and Mathematics, Sociology and Economics slightly higher at 6.3\%, 7\% and 7.7\%, respectively. The overall pattern highlights a predominance of leading authorships across all four field datasets.

Lastly, we emphasize the effects of publications and $T^\prime$-index reflecting the leading roles of two authors sharing the same research sub-field with identical publication counts by participation in Table~\ref{comparison_t_index}.
While both authors have published $74$ papers, the right-hand side author's $T^\prime$-index is significantly lower, $0.48$, compared to the left-hand side author, $0.95$.
That is, the author on the right-hand side took more supporting roles down in the byline than the author on the left-hand side.

