By Lemma~\ref{lem:basis=factors1} it suffices to show that any vector $\vb*{v}\in\Z^n$ is an integer linear combination of columns of $A$.
Choose any point $p\in S$.
Then the points $p$ and $p+\vb*{v}$ are connected in the lifted periodic graph $G\subset\R^n$ by a polygonal path of straight-line edges.
Under $G\to G/\La$, this path projects to a closed cycle $C$ at the vertex ($\La$-equivalence class) $p+\La$ in the labelled quotient graph $Q=G/\La$.
\smallskip

Let the cycle $C$ pass through edges $e_1,\dots,e_k$ (with integer multiplicities) in the complement $Q-F$ of the forest $F$ in the quotient graph $Q$. 
These edges were added only to $Q$ in case 2 of the loop stage.
When we tried to add every edge $e_j$ to $F$, the edge $e_j$ created a cycle $C_j$ whose cycle sum appeared as a column in the translational matrix $A$ (if this cycle sum was not yet an integer combination of the previous columns).
Then the vector $\vb*{v}$ equals the sum of the cycle sums of all the cycles $C_j$ for $j=1,\dots,k$, which is an integer combination of the columns of $A$ as required. 
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}[connected $G/\La$ $\Rightarrow\exists$ a tree of representatives $T\subset G$]
\label{lem:tree_representatives}
%As translational basis vectors are only added when they form a cycle with tree edges, we can convert this lemma description to the following:
%Within every Periodic Spanning Graph, there exists a subtree in $G$ of $m$ points, with no $\lambda$-equivalent pairs, connected by $m-1$ straight line edges.
%Any connected periodic graph $G\subset\R^n$ on a periodic point set $S$ with a motif of $m$ points and a lattice $\La$ includes a straight-line tree $T$ on $m$ points of $S$ that are not $\La$-equivalent to each other.
If a labelled quotient graph $Q=G/\La$ is connected, its lifted graph $G\subset\R^n$ on a periodic point set $S$ with a motif of $m$ points and a lattice $\La$ includes a straight-line \emph{tree of representatives} $T\subset G$ whose $m$ vertices are not $\La$-equivalent to each other.
%Then the quotient tree $T/\La$ makes the quotient graph $G/\La$ connected.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
\begin{comment}
    From any Periodic Spanning Graph, we can build such a tree:
If a periodic graph is spanning, then, for any pair of points in a unit cell, there exist a path to connect them. There is no need for any of these paths to include multiple examples of any $\lambda$-equivalent points, as the periodicity of the point set (and our only requirement being connectedness of any one set of representatives) means we can alter the paths to never feature more than one representative of a motif point (by removing one representative and all edges in between them). A union of all such paths between motif points can be reduced to a tree by removing edges that form cycles.
 %add a-b-b-c idea
If such a tree cannot be found, then there exists some pair $(p,q)$ that are not connected by a path, therefore the periodic graph is not spanning%, proving property 1.
\end{comment}
%If $G$ is connected, then so is its quotient graph $G/\La$.
Since $Q$ is connected, we can choose a spanning tree $F\subset Q$ on the $m$ vertices of $Q$.
%Take a minimum spanning tree $\MST(S/\La)$ on the $m$-vertex set of $G/\La$, see Definition~\ref{dfn:tree}.
The required tree $T\subset G$ will be a connected union of straight-line edges of $G$ that map 1-1 to all edges of $F$ under the quotient $G\to Q$.
Start from any point $p\in S$ and take any edge $e$ at the vertex ($\La$-equivalence class) $p+\La$ of $F\subset Q$.
The preimage of $e$ under $G\to Q$ contains a unique straight-line edge $[p,q]\subset G$, which we add to $T$.
After adding to $T$ all edges at $p$ that project to all edges of $F$ at $p+\La$,
choose another point $p'\in T$ such that the vertex $p'+\La$ has an edge of $F$ not yet covered by $T$ under $G\to Q$.
We continue adding edges to $T$ by using their projections in $F\subset Q$ until we get a full tree $T\subset G$ that spans all $m$ non-$\La$-equivalent points of $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}[termination conditions in Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length} $\Rightarrow$ connected $G$]
\label{lem:sufficiency}
Let $Q$ be a labelled quotient graph with a translational matrix $A$ and a lifted graph $G$ on a periodic point set $S\subset\R^n$ with a lattice $\La$.
If $Q$ is connected and the matrix $A$ has $n$ invariant factors equal to 1, then the lifted periodic graph $G$ is connected.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
For any points $p,q\in S$, we find a path of straight-line edges in $G$ as follows.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:tree_representatives}, the connectedness of the quotient graph $Q=G/\La$ guarantees a tree $T\subset G$ whose vertices represent all $\La$-equivalences classes of points of $S$.
Let $p',q'$ be the vertices of $T$ that are $\La$-equivalent to $p,q$, respectively.
\smallskip

Since $p',q'$ are connected by a path in $T$, it suffices to find a path from $p$ to its $\La$-translate $p'=p+\vb*{v}$ (then similarly from $q$ to $q'$) in the graph $G$ for any $\vb*{v}\in\La$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:basis=factors1} the columns of $A$ form a basis of $\Z^n$, so $\vb*{v}$ is an integer combination of these columns.
It remains to find a path in $G$ by assuming that $\vb*{v}$ is one column of $A$.
This column can appear in $A$ only in case 2 of the loop stage in Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length} as a cycle sum of a cycle $C\subset Q$ that was created by trying to add an edge $e$ from Algorithm~\ref{alg:edges} to a forest $F\subset Q$.
If we order all edges of $C$ from the vertex $p+\La$ as $e_1,\dots,e_k$, the sum of their translation vectors equals $\vb*{v}$.
We build a path from $p$ to $p+\vb*{v}$ in $G$ by finding a unique edge $[p,p_1]\subset G$ that projects to $e_1$, then a unique edge $[p_1,p_2]\subset G$ that projects to $e_2$ and so on until we cover all $e_1,\dots,e_k$ and arrive at $p+\vb*{v}$. 
\end{proof}

