\begin{proof}[\textbf{Proof of Theorem}~\ref{thm:bridge_length_time}]
Algorithm \ref{alg:bridge_length} has the initialisation of a constant time $O(1)$ and the loop stage.
We will multiply an upper bound for the number of loops by the time complexity of each loop.
% (The complexity of algorithm 1 will be shown to be dominated by algorithm 2).
One loop in Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length} consists of the checks below. 
    \begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{(Check1)} Does adding an edge $e$ to a forest $F$ create a cycle? %if it is$\dots$
    \item \emph{(Check2)} Is the cycle sum an integer combination of previous cycle sums? %and if not$\dots$
    \item \emph{(CheckT)} After appending a cycle sum $c$ to the matrix $A$ and %(in the naive case) 
    calculating the Smith Normal Form of $A$, does $A$ have $n$ invariant factors equal to 1?
    \end{itemize}

 \emph{Check1} and \emph{Check2} can be done in $O(m^2)$ time, by using an antisymmetric additive matrix $R$, where $R_{ij}$ is the sum for the path from $p_i$ to $p_j$ in the LQG $Q$. 
\smallskip

In the naive case,  \emph{CheckT} has complexity $O(N+m^2)=O(N)$, where $O(N)$ is the complexity of the Smith Normal Form. 
Since this term is dominant, we will use $O(N)$ to represent the complexity of a single loop iteration of Algorithm \ref{alg:bridge_length}.
 The complexity of updating the matrix $R$ is dominated by steps that have $O(N)$ complexity.
\smallskip

   A different way of performing \emph{CheckT} is to append columns to $A$ in an 'online' fashion.
This avoids the need to calculate the Smith Normal Form from scratch every time (or often at all), and reduces the complexity to a time close to $O(Mul\cdot E\cdot n)$, where $O(Mul)=O(m^{\log_2 7})$ is the complexity of multiplication of $m\times m$ matrices, see \cite{karstadt2020matrix}, and $O(E)$ is the complexity of the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. 
As this reduction in complexity is dominated by the price of populating the edges with Algorithm \ref{alg:edges}, this will be irrelevant for most use cases (and is not used in the experiments shown later).
If a use case involves, say, a large or high-dimensional pre-populated set of edges, then more information can be found in Appendix \ref{app:SNF}.%see the code at *** 
\smallskip

Every loop iteration calls Algorithm~\ref{alg:edges}.
If we consider all calls to Algorithm~\ref{alg:edges} as running sequentially, then the main loop will run at most $a(U)+1$ times, where $a(U)$ is the aspect ratio from Definition~\ref{dfn:rURS}. 
Each loop runs through the unit cells that are '$supercell\_size$' away from the central cell $U_1$.
By the end, we will have run through and \emph{yielded} $(a(U)+1)^n$ unit cells. 
For each unit cell $U_i$, we find all distances between the $m$ points in $U_i$ and $m$ points in the central cell.
The required time is $O(m^2)$ for two cells and $O(m^2a(U)^n)$ for all cells.
%, with an output of $a(U)^n\cdot m^2$ edges, therefore the same number of loop iterations for Algorithm 2.
\smallskip

Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length} does not actually run for every edge found by Algorithm~\ref{alg:edges} but we assume this for simplicity. 
All other operations in Algorithm~\ref{alg:edges} are dominated by the complexity $O(N)$ for each iteration of Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length}, for example checking if $Q$ is connected, which requires $O(m)$ time by using the matrix $R$.        
    %\\(Even in our improved algorithm, (that swaps an $O(N)$ step for an $O(mul\cdot E\cdot n)$ step), $O(mul)$ still dominates $O(m^2)$) 
The worst-case complexity for the naive implementation of Algorithm~\ref{alg:bridge_length} is $O(m^2a(U)^nN)$.
\end{proof}


