\section*{Applications}
We can treat stochastic $\mathbb{Z}$-linear eigenvalue matrices as generator matrices by subtracting their row-sums along the diagonal, allowing us to adopt a multidimensional Markov chain formalism\cite{MDMC} often used in stochastic automata networks (SAN). 
\begin{figure}[H]
\[ Q = Q_{0} + Q_{D}, \;\; Q_0 = \sum_{k=1}^{K}\bigotimes_{h = 1}^{H} Q_{k}^{(h)}, \;\; Q_{D} = -diag{(Q_{0}e)}\]
\[Q_{local} =\sum_{k=1}^{H}\bigotimes_{h = 1}^{H} Q_{k}^{(h)}, \; Q_{synchronized} = \sum_{k=H+1}^{K}\bigotimes_{h = 1}^{H} Q_{k}^{(h)}\]
\caption{MDMC. $e$: the $\mathbf{1}$s vector, $H$: $\#$ factors in each term, $K$: $\#$ terms. Subsystems $1, \hdots, H$ model local transitions, $H+1, \hdots, K$ model synchronized transitions.}
\label{fig:oooo}
\end{figure}

\noindent Such models are relevant because of the properties of tensor products and sums\cite{tp}. For eigenvalues $\lambda$ and $\mu$ of $A$ and $B$ and their eigenvectors $x$ and $y$, $A \otimes B$ contains eigenvalues $\lambda \mu$ paired with eigenvectors $x \otimes y$. Eigenvalues of $A \oplus B$ come in the form $\lambda + \mu$ with eigenvectors $x \otimes y$. Since the probability transition matrix $P$ is the matrix exponential of the generator matrix $G$, $P=e^{Q}Q(0)$, we can see how the term 'local' comes from the capacity to factor the exponential and isolate the first $H$ terms, while synchronized transitions couple together due to the eigenvalues being products rather than sums. By treating $M^{X}$ matrices as building blocks in the MDMC formalism we can easily trace spectral contributions from each factor.
\newline\\
One simple way to get generators from these matrices is to add $Q_D = -diag(Q_0e)$. We can build multidimensional models with local and synchronous transitions in any fashion and make sure rows sum to 0. Consider two matrices $A$ and $B$ - 
