\section{Conclusion and future work}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This work introduces failure divergence refinement for Event-B, enabling preservation assumptions about behavioral properties during refinement. Modelers can conduct the refinement check push-button through the algorithm and tool presented, contrasting previous methods requiring additional proofs. This offers swift, early feedback, facilitating the trickle-down of validation results along refinement chains without redoing validation. %, thus promoting time savings and early validation.

In the future, we aim to integrate the tool into ProB2-Ui~\cite{Bendisposto2021} for accessibility. As discussed in \Cref{sec:formalization}, an extension to LTL (or even CTL) is possible, and we plan to explore this. Furthermore, investigating readiness refinement/bisimulation (cf.~\cite{DeRoever1998}) is an intriguing avenue. Eshuis and Fokkinga~\cite{Eshuis2002} have observed a strong resemblance between failure divergence refinement and bisimulation for LTS, which warrants further exploration.

%Besides making the proposed tool more accessible, e.g., by integrating it deeper in ProB or ProB2-Ui~\cite{Bendisposto2021}) and robust against manipulating parameters, an investigation into what failure divergence refinement else can preserve is warranted. A possible future extension of this work could be those cases where stuttering steps were introduced. Stock et al.~\cite{Stock2022a} worked on this for individual traces. However, a global solution may also be desirable. Furthermore, one can look into the applicability to $LTL$ where \cite{Hoang2016,Zhu2023} tackled the problem already solely based on proof obligations, $CTL$ or $CTL^{*}$ where Derrick and Graeme~\cite{Derrick2012} tackled the problem for Z. Furthermore, it might be interesting to investigate whether, for Event-B, there is a difference between failure divergence refinement and readiness refinement/bisimulation (see Roever and Engelhardt~\cite{DeRoever1998} for an overview of the formalism). In this context, it is interesting that Eshuis and Fokkinga~\cite{Eshuis2002} found a strong similarity between failure divergence refinement and bisimulation for LTS.


