\mypar{DP Text Generation via the Exponential Mechanism}
Our goal is to design an algorithm $\calA(\bfq, \bfR)$ to generate text in response to a given query $\bfq$ and references $\bfR$ with a desired $\rho$-zCDP guarantee. We assume the \textbf{whitebox setting} where model logits $\phi(\,\cdot \, | \, \bfx)$ can be queried for any $\bfx \in V^*$. This is in contrast to the API-access setting, where one inference call returns an entire text sequence~\cite{xie2024dpsyndata}.
%

We describe the prior state-of-the-art approach of \citet{amin2024private}, which generates the next token $x_t \in V$ via the canonical exponential mechanism~\cite{mcsherry2007exp} over the next-token logits of the given LLM.
%
Given a query $\bfq$ and sensitive references $\bfR$, the first step is to obtain the next-token logits $\bfphi_i = \phi(\cdot\, |\, \bfq, \bfr_i, \bfxlt) \in \R^{|V|}$ for each $i \in [\bsz]$ with an LLM inference call. Second, we clip and aggregate the logits as $\bar\bfphi = (1/\bsz) \sum_{i=1}^\bsz \clip_\clipnorm(\bfphi_i)$, where $\clip_\clipnorm(\bfphi)$ clips each coordinate of $\bfphi_i$ to lie in $[-C, C]$.\footnote{
    The clipping employed by \citet{amin2024private} incorporates a re-centering step, as logits are invariant to additive shifts. While it is shown to be empirically important, we omit this detail in our exposition as it does not change its mathematical properties.
}
We can then sample a $\rho$-zCDP token $x_t \in V$ by sampling~\cite{expmechdporg2021rogers}
\begin{align} \label{eq:expo-mech}
    Q(x_t \, | \, \bfq, \bfR, \bfxlt) \propto \exp\left( \tfrac{\sqrt{2\rho}}{\sens(\bar\bfphi)} \cdot \bar \bfphi(x_t) \right) 
\end{align}
where the sensitivity $\sens(\bar\bfphi)$ measures the maximum change in $\bar\bfphi(y)$, when we move to an adjacent set $\bfR' \simeq \bfR$ of references for any $y \in V$. The clipping and aggregating steps control the sensitivity as $\sens(\bar\bfphi) = O(\clipnorm / \bsz)$,\footnote{
We have $\sens(\bar\bfphi)=2\clipnorm/\bsz$ (resp. $\clipnorm/\bsz$) under replace-by-null (resp. zero-out) adjacency; cf. \S\ref{sec:review}. We give this method an advantage in \S\ref{sec:expt} by (incorrectly) taking $\sens(\bar\bfphi)=\clipnorm/\bsz$ under replace-by-null adjacency.
} 
so that \cref{eq:expo-mech} reduces to sampling with temperature $\tau = O(\clipnorm / \bsz\sqrt{\rho})$.

The clip norm $\clipnorm$ is a tunable parameter. If it is too small, the resulting distortions in the next-token logits boost the probabilities of the unlikely tail tokens, see \Cref{fig:clip}. On the other hand, if $\clipnorm$ is too large, the temperature $\tau \propto \clipnorm/\bsz$ becomes too large. Both scenarios lead to degenerate text. 

Thus, generating non-degenerate text from this method requires taking $\clipnorm$ sufficiently large (e.g. \cite{amin2024private} recommend $\clipnorm\approx10$), and $\bsz$ large enough to ensure a temperature of $\tau = O(\clipnorm / \bsz\sqrt{\rho}) \approx 1$. That is, generating each private token requires a large number $\bsz$ of LLM next-token inference calls.\footnote{
    In practice, we can typically control $\bsz$, as this can be the number of reference documents we use for one synthetic generation, or the number of retrieved references in RAG systems.
} 
%
We find in \S\ref{sec:expt} that this method fails to produce coherent text at $\bsz < O(50)$. 

Finally, \citet{amin2024private} adaptively use tokens generated from public logits $\bfphi_\public = \phi(\,\cdot \,|\, \bfq, \bfxlt)$ using the sparse vector technique (SVT)~\citep{dwork2010boosting} if they are close enough to the aggregated private logits $\bar \bfphi$.
While this improves the overall privacy-utility tradeoffs, it yields a data-dependent DP guarantee, making it hard to calibrate the noise level to a desired privacy guarantee in advance. 

Next, we describe how \method overcomes these drawbacks.

