\subsection{Top-$k$ Selection}
Some additional statistics of selection from the expanded vocabulary described by the \ourtopk step of \ours are presented in \Cref{tab:topk_stats}. In particular, we show the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Effective $k$: the size of the vocabulary $V_k^+$ averaged over all generated tokens.
    \item \#toks: This is the number of tokens generated from the expanded vocabulary $V_k^+ \setminus V_k$ for a given generation; we report the average over all generations.
\end{itemize}

As the privacy budget increases, the clipping norm $\clipnorm$ also increases. We observe that the larger the expansion of the top-$k$ threshold ($2\clipnorm/\bsz$), the larger the effective-$k$ ($|\Vtopk^+|$) of \ourtopk sampling. Further, as reported in \S\ref{sec:expt}, we also observe that the number of tokens sampled from the expanded set is very low, supporting the claim that $\Vtopk^+$ is a very tight superset of $\Vtopk$. The number of sampled tokens also decreases as $k$ increases. Despite a larger increase in effective $k$ due to the expansion of the top-$k$ vocabulary, the probability of selection of tokens decreases sharply with rank since the increased expansion is offset by lower probabilities of selection.

