\subsection{Ablation Study}
\subsubsection{BHCCM}

In the ablation study of BHCCM, we validate the effectiveness of its internal design of bidirectional semantic consistency constraints as well as the proposed loss function $\mathcal{L}_\text{HSC}$. As shown in Table~\ref{ablation-BHCCM-table}, we use ConvNeXt-Base with a flat classification structure as the baseline, which achieves an mIoU of 73.73\% and an mAcc of 80.06\% on the GaoFen-2 data of MM-5B dataset. We design five comparative settings: 

(1) \textbf{Without fusion} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{HCE}}$): mIoU = 71.36\%, using three independent 2D convolutions generated the final results, led to a significant performance drop, decreasing by 2.37\% compared to the baseline. 

(2) \textbf{Coarse-to-fine} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{HCE}}$): mIoU = 72.57\%, improves by 1.21\% compared to setting 1), but still falls short of the baseline by 1.16\%. 

(3) \textbf{Fine-to-coarse} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{HCE}}$): mIoU = 72.55\%, which is still 1.18\% lower than the baseline. 

(4) \textbf{Bidirectional} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{HCE}}$): mIoU = 73.75\%, improved performance by 2.39\% compared to the 1) setting, essentially restoring it to baseline levels. 

(5) \textbf{Bidirectional} ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{HSC}}$): mIoU = 74.77\%. Finally, on top of the bidirectional information fusin, we integrated the proposed hierarchical semantic consistency constraint $\mathcal{L}_{\text{HSC}}$. Under this setting, the mIoU reached 74.77\%, and the mAcc improved to 81.15\%, showing improvements of 1.04\% and 1.09\% over the baseline, respectively. 

These results clearly demonstrate that the bidirectional semantic consistency constraints and the hierarchical semantic consistency loss in BHCCM significantly enhance the performance of hierarchical LCLU classification.

