\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
We evaluate \ourmodelacronym~on GPT-Large and Phi-2. To thoroughly evaluate its ability to handle long-context sequences, we use LongBench \cite{bai2024longbench}, which comprises 21 real-world and synthetic long-context tasks.

We begin by examining the improvements in long-context capabilities of the studied models with \ourmodelacronym~and full fine-tuning at test-time. Next, we investigate whether the test-time training overhead can be reduced. Specifically, we demonstrate that: 1) Fine-tuning only the up-projection layers in the feed-forward networks (also known as key \cite{geva2021transformer}) can further improve accuracy compare to full fine-tuning while reducing the number of trainable parameters by approximately 70\%.
2) We find that restricting fine-tuning to only the deeper layers allows us to reduce the number of trainable parameters at test-time to just 15\% of the model's parameters, with little to no loss in performance.


\paragraph{Experimental details.}
In all of the experiments, we chunk the long-context input into subsequences of 512 tokens with an overlap of 32 tokens between adjacent chunks. For each input, we fine-tune the model for 10 epochs and restore the original model parameters after inference. We adopt the Adam optimizer with a learning
rate of $5e^{-4}$ and weight decay of $0.5$.





\subsection{\ourmodelacronym~Enhances Long-Context Understanding Across Standard Long-Context Tasks}

Figure~\ref{fig:scaleLaw} shows the impact of \ourmodelacronym~on the long-context understanding capabilities of Phi-2 and GPT-Large, plotted as a function of the  context length. In all the experiments, the  context $\llminput$ is truncate in the middle following \cite{bai2024longbench}. In these experiments, we applied full fine-tuning at test-time and reported the average LongBench score across all 21 tasks. We observe that performance consistently improves across all LongBench tasks as the context length increases.
