\subsection{The shallower key layers are less effective compared to the deeper layers
}
We also empirically investigate the effectiveness of fine-tuning shallower $\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}$ layers at test-time. If we freeze a block of shalloest layer and observe no impact on \ourmodelacronym's performance, it suggests that the layers are not essential for \ourmodelacronym. To identify the optimal block of shallow layers to freeze, we incrementally freeze shallow layers and evaluate \ourmodelacronym's performance at each step. This bottom-up strategy reduces the number of trainable parameters and computational cost, as backpropagation is not required for the shallow frozen layers.

Figure~\ref{fig:prunning} shows  \ourmodelacronym's average LongBench score as the fraction of shallow key ($\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}$) layers frozen. We observe that fine-tuning only the top 80\% of $\text{FFN}_{\text{Up}}$ layers achieves the similar performance as fine-tuning all layers. Importantly, there is a sharp performance degradation when freezing more than 40\% of the shallow layers, indicating a transition point beyond which key contextual information is no longer preserved.


