Figure~\ref{fig:hacc_scaling} shows that execution time, represented by the bars on the left y-axis, is consistently lower for HBM compared to DDR across all tested node counts. As the node count increases, HBM continues to exhibit faster execution times than DDR. However, DDR demonstrates better scaling efficiency, particularly at larger node counts.

The scaling efficiency results, shown as lines on the right y-axis, indicate that both memory systems maintain high efficiency as the node count increases. At 1024 nodes, both HBM and DDR achieve approximately 99\% scaling efficiency (relative to 128 nodes). As the system scales to 8192 nodes, DDR sustains above 99\% efficiency, while HBM efficiency decreases to 97\%. This divergence suggests that the longer computation time of DDR allows for more effective overlap with communication tasks, enabling higher efficiency at larger scales. Nonetheless, both HBM and DDR exhibit excellent scaling efficiency across the tested configurations.
