\section{Introduction}

\begin{frame}{Understanding the Evolution of Computational Social Science}
    \begin{itemize}[<+-| alert@+>] % stepwise alerts
        \item Computational Social Science (CSS) integrates computational methods with traditional social science, driving a paradigm shift in research. Initially focused on simulations in the 1990s, CSS transitioned to data-driven approaches in the 2000s, leveraging internet and social media data. Using NLP on 11 million papers, we analyze the evolution of CSS and its transformative impact on social sciences.
        \item Interactive and animated visualizations showcase CSS’s temporal evolution and influence on adjacent fields. These tools enable researchers to intuitively explore patterns, revealing trends, boundary shifts, and knowledge diffusion across disciplines.
    \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\section{Data}
\begin{frame}{MAG Data}
    We analyzed 10,981,104 English-language papers from the Microsoft Academic Graph (MAG) dataset spanning 1990–2021, covering sociology (17.79 \%), economics (16.94 \%), political science (28.17 \%), and psychology (37.09 \% ). Non-English papers were excluded using fastText. This dataset provides a comprehensive view of major social sciences during the active development of CSS.
\end{frame}

