However, these foundational models are widely known for their massive model sizes and reliance on extensive and diverse datasets sourced from books, web pages, and other domains for training~\cite{zhou2024comprehensive}. As a result, they inherently face limitations, including challenges related to data privacy, computational resource constraints, and limited adaptability to domain-specific tasks without further fine-tuning~\cite{lin2023pushing}. First, training the GPT-3 model with 175 billion parameters requires 1,024 Nvidia V100 GPUs running for 34 days at an estimated cost of \$4.6 million US dollars~\cite{zhao2024optimizing}, which is an expense that is prohibitive for emerging companies with limited computational resources and financial support. Moreover, end users are often reluctant to share private data for training and are concerned about its potential misuse by large models like GPT~\cite{wu2024unveiling}. Second, training data may lack comprehensive global representation, leading to inherent biases. For instance, models may reinforce stereotypes by associating men with engineers roles and women with caregiving tasks or by favoring racial majorities over people of other ethnic backgrounds in professional recommendations~\cite{lyu2023pathway}. Third, new data are continuously generated over time~\cite{aggarwal2023chameleon}, making it challenging to keep large models up-to-date. Retraining or even fine-tuning these models with incremental data is highly costly and poses significant difficulties for end users who lack the resources or expertise to modify them. Fourth, personalized applications require models that can adapt to individual users' preferences and unique data patterns. For instance, a healthcare platform may need to offer tailored diagnoses based on a user's medical history, while an e-commerce recommendation system should adjust to personalized shopping behaviors. Therefore, traditional centralized foundation model training struggles to meet these needs, as it needs to balance AGI ambitions with the requirement for privacy-preserving personalized intelligence.


In this paper, we propose a new vision that complements AGI, called artificial personalized intelligence (API), which emphasizes providing users with customized intelligent services. To realize this vision, we introduce personalized federated intelligence (PFI), a framework that combines federated learning (FL)~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} and foundational model technologies, aiming to achieve personalized intelligence while ensuring data privacy. Note that FL is a decentralized paradigm that enables model training on distributed data without requiring data sharing~\cite{qiao2024federated,mcmahan2017communication,10531097,nguyen2025semspace,qiao2024logit,qiao2025towards}. Unlike traditional personalized federated learning~\cite{tan2022towards,qiao2024fedccl,deng2020adaptive}, PFI focuses on collaboratively training FMs from scratch across distributed entities, followed by post-training or fine-tuning to adapt the models to each entity's specific domain. In other words, we envision a central server in PFI that no longer performs centralized training, but instead coordinates multiple distributed entities with heterogeneous settings and private data to collaboratively develop a shared foundation model. Following this, each entity applies domain-specific adjustments to refine the model, resulting in a personalized foundation model tailored to their unique needs. However, it is worthwhile to note that while distributed foundation model training offers scalability and privacy protection benefits, challenges such as communication costs due to bandwidth limitations, heterogeneity arising from differences in systems, models, data, and environments, as well as potential attacks from adversaries, will pose significant challenges to the realization of PFI. Therefore, this paper aims to explore the motivations, challenges, potential solutions, and future directions of PFI, providing a comprehensive overview and analysis of the field while envisioning the future of the API era.




\subsection{Major Contributions}
This survey represents the first comprehensive exploration that envisions an era where API complements the widely discussed AGI. We provide an in-depth discussion of the enabling technologies, which we refer to as personalized federated intelligence (PFI), aimed at achieving both strong generalization and user-specific personalization while ensuring privacy protection for resource-constrained end-users. Table~\ref{tab_tease_discussion} provides a comparison of the topics, contributions, and remarks between our survey and existing ones, focusing on FL, FMs, and their intersection. However, most existing surveys primarily focus on general aspects of FL~\cite{zhang2021survey}, mobile edge networks~\cite{9060868}, non-IID challenges~\cite{zhu2021federated}, and specific applications, such as medical imaging~\cite{guan2024federated} or IoT~\cite{9415623}. While a few studies~\cite{zhuang2023foundation,ren2025advances,federatedlearningfoundationmodel} have begun to explore the role of FMs within the context of FL, most of these works focus on leveraging their strong zero-shot capabilities to boost FL generalization performance, often overlooking the critical need for end-user-level personalization. In contrast, our survey positions PFI as a key enabler of the API era, and provides a comprehensive examination of how foundation models can be effectively integrated into FL systems, with particular emphasis on personalized model adaptation, efficient computation and communication strategies, and trustworthy training and inference under privacy and security constraints. Beyond this, we further explore emerging future directions, including Meta-PFI, defined as the “user–agent–world” interaction ecosystem, quantum-enabled PFI, and sustainable and green PFI, all of which further highlight the central role of PFI in shaping future API systems. 

Overall, the key contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize} 
\item[$\bullet$] With the goal of advancing an API era that complements the development of AGI, this survey aims to shift focus towards the personalization aspect by exploring PFI, which, in contrast to prior work that has mainly concentrated on the role of FMs in enhancing the generalization capabilities of FL models, emphasizes the importance of end-user personalization.
\item[$\bullet$] To provide a clear understanding of both FL and FMs, we revisit the key enabling technologies and the taxonomy of FL, and propose a new taxonomy for foundation models, highlighting their synergies and identifying key gaps that motivate the integration of personalization into federated foundation model frameworks.
\item[$\bullet$] To lay the groundwork for practical PFI deployment, we outline key technical directions for building PFI systems, including retrieval-augmented generation, efficient adaptation strategies, and mechanisms to ensure trustworthiness under real-world constraints such as privacy, security, and limited resources.
\item[$\bullet$] Looking ahead, we outline a forward-looking research agenda encompassing Meta-PFI, quantum-enabled PFI, and sustainable intelligence, aiming to inspire future exploration towards next-generation AI systems that are user-centric and well-suited for decentralized, privacy-sensitive environments.
\end{itemize}


