\subsection{Organization of This Paper}
The remainder of this survey is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec_background} presents an overview of FL, including its taxonomy based on participant roles and data distribution. It also reviews FMs, categorized by modality, architecture, training paradigms, and deployment settings. This section further outlines the motivation for bridging FL and FMs, highlighting the shift towards privacy-preserving, resource-efficient intelligence that reduces reliance on centralized infrastructure. 



Building on these objectives, we organize the paper as in Figure~\ref{structure}: Section~\ref{sec:motivation_PFI} goes beyond traditional federated FMs to explore the motivation for PFI, where federated foundation models serve as the basic framework but require the incorporation of additional techniques for personalization. Section~\ref{sec_opportunity_PFI} delves into opportunities for advancing PFI, focusing on retrieval augmentation, efficiency, and trustworthiness. Section~\ref{sec_challenges_future} identifies key challenges such as heterogeneity, communication overhead, privacy and security, as well as the trade-off between personalization and generalization. It also outlines future directions in this emerging field, including the proposal of concepts such as meta-PFI, quantum-enabled PFI, and sustainable and green PFI. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the survey.



