To provide a clear and systematic classification of the foundation model paradigm, we propose a taxonomy that organizes FMs across four key dimensions. First, based on the modality of input data, FMs can be classified into two types: unimodal models, which are trained on a single modality such as text (e.g., BERT~\cite{lewis2019bart}), vision (e.g., MAE~\cite{he2022masked}), or audio (e.g., Wav2Vec~\cite{schneider2019wav2vec}); and multimodal models, which are designed to process and integrate information from multiple modalities, such as image-text (e.g. CLIP~\cite{radford2021learning}) or video-language (e.g. VideoBERT~\cite{sun2019videobert}) pairs. Second, from the perspective of model architecture, FMs can be categorized into three types: dense models, which activate all parameters during both training and inference (e.g., BERT)~\cite{riquelme2021scaling}; sparse models, such as mixture-of-experts (MoE), where only a subset of parameters is activated for each input to improve efficiency and scalability (e.g., GLaM~\cite{du2022glam},, DeepSeekMoE~\cite{dai2024deepseekmoe}); and retriever-augmented models, which leverage external memory or retrieval mechanisms to enhance factual accuracy and knowledge utilization (e.g., RETRO~\cite{borgeaud2022improving}, RAG~\cite{lewis2020retrieval}). Third, from the perspective of training paradigms, FMs can be broadly categorized into two groups. The first category comprises self-supervised pretraining models, which learn general-purpose representations from large-scale unlabeled data by solving pretext tasks such as masked token prediction or contrastive learning (e.g., MAE~\cite{he2022masked}, BERT~\cite{lewis2019bart}). The second category encompasses reinforcement learning (RL)-based training strategies, including reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)~\cite{ouyang2022training}, reinforcement learning from AI feedback (RLAIF)~\cite{bai2022constitutional}, and pure RL~\cite{guo2025deepseek} approaches. These methods aim to improve alignment with user intent and enhance the safety and robustness of models in real-world applications. For instance, InstructGPT is trained using RLHF, where human preference data serves as a reward signal to fine-tune the model’s behavior~\cite{ouyang2022training}. Claude leverages RLAIF, in which a preference model is used as the reward function without any human labels~\cite{bai2022constitutional}. DeepSeek-R1-Zero, on the other hand, is trained via large-scale pure RL without any supervised fine-tuning stages such as RLHF or RLAIF, serving as an early exploration into instruction-following driven purely by RL~\cite{guo2025deepseek}. Finally, with respect to deployment and usage scenarios, FMs can be classified into two broad categories: cloud-centric models, which are typically deployed on centralized infrastructure and accessed via APIs; and on-device models, which are optimized for local inference, personalization, and privacy-preserving applications. For instance, GPT-4 and Claude are cloud-based models that offer powerful capabilities through API access, enabling large-scale applications across various domains. In contrast, models such as Meta’s on-device TinyLLaMA~\cite{zhang2024tinyllama} and Google’s Gemini Nano~\cite{team2023gemini} are specifically designed to run directly on edge devices, such as smartphones, offering reduced latency and improved data privacy. This taxonomy offers a systematic framework for analyzing the capabilities, design trade-offs, and deployment scenarios of FMs, with a comparative overview presented in Table~\ref{tab:fm-taxonomy}.







\subsection{Foundation Model Meets Federated Learning}


Although FMs demonstrate impressive capabilities across a wide range of tasks, they pose significant challenges, particularly in scenarios that require data privacy, personalization, and on-device adaptation~\cite{jia202310}. Additionally, small companies or individual end-users often struggle to access the large-scale datasets and computational resources needed to train FMs tailored to their specific needs. One emerging direction is the development of lightweight FMs that can be deployed on resource-constrained devices, as exemplified by recent efforts such as TinyLLaMA~\cite{zhang2024tinyllama}, Nano~\cite{team2023gemini}, and Phi~\cite{abdin2024phi}. These efforts highlight the growing interest in implementing efficient on-device FMs. However, this approach presents a fundamental trade-off, as reducing the number of parameters to fit limited computational resources may inevitably weaken the model’s inherent strengths in scale and powerful representational capacity~\cite{rokh2023comprehensive}. Meanwhile, challenges still persist in acquiring large-scale data and computing resources, as well as in achieving scalability, privacy, and personalization. In this context, FL emerges as a promising approach to address these challenges associated with FMs, paving the way for the federated foundation models (FFMs) paradigm. Unlike traditional centralized training approaches, FL enables collaborative learning across multiple devices or organizations without requiring direct access to raw data~\cite{mcmahan2017communication, yang2019federated}. This is particularly advantageous in scenarios involving sensitive user data, such as personal texts, medical images, or proprietary business information, that cannot be shared due to privacy regulations or ethical considerations~\cite{razi2025enhancing,10049010}. Therefore, when integrated with FMs, FL offers a decentralized alternative for both pre-training and fine-tuning, enabling models to learn from heterogeneous and distributed data sources while preserving user privacy. Several recent studies have explored integrating FMs into the FL framework. For instance, FATE-LLM~\cite{fan2023fate} explores privacy-preserving LLMs in federated settings by coordinating fine-tuning across multiple clients. OpenFedLLM~\cite{ye2024openfedllm} adopts a similar approach by training LLMs on decentralized private data through the federated training paradigm. Overall, these efforts highlight a promising shift towards privacy-preserving and resource-efficient FMs that move away from dependence on centralized infrastructures.



\section{Motivation for Personalized Federated Intelligence}
\label{sec:motivation_PFI}

While FL offers a promising decentralized and privacy-preserving paradigm for training FMs, it faces fundamental challenges in adapting to the diverse data distributions and resource constraints across participants~\cite{le2024cross}. In real-world scenarios, participant data is often non-independent and identically distributed, as each user or device typically exhibits unique characteristics influenced by factors such as geographic location, individual behavior, and personal preferences~\cite{le2025mitigating,sabah2025fairdpfl}. For instance, typing behaviors on mobile keyboards differ across users, smartwatch health data varies with individual physiology and routines, and corporate logs capture unique operational patterns across organizations~\cite{sivakumar2024emg2qwerty}. Consequently, such heterogeneity poses a significant challenge for traditional FL methods, which optimize a single global model that may fail to capture this diversity, ultimately resulting in suboptimal performance on local tasks and diminished user experiences~\cite{li2020federated}. This highlights a fundamental limitation in training a shared global foundation model within federated settings and motivates the need for personalized federated intelligence (PFI), where models are adapted to better align with the unique data distributions of each client. By facilitating both collaboration and personalization, PFI strikes a balance between generalization and customization, making it especially well-suited for FMs deployed in personalized settings such as mobile assistants, healthcare applications, and intelligent IoT systems~\cite{banabilah2022federated,kishor2022personalized}. Moreover, with the growing popularity of lightweight FMs such as OpenAI o1-mini~\cite{wang2024ontheplanning}, Lite-SAM~\cite{fu2024lite}, and DeepSeek-V2-Lite~\cite{liu2024deepseek}, PFI becomes a crucial mechanism to adapt these models efficiently to diverse user environments without requiring full model retraining or centralized fine-tuning.


The objective of PFI is to obtain a series of models $\Omega = \{ \omega_1, \omega_2, ..., \omega_K \}$, where each model $\omega_k$ is owned by one of the $K$ participants. Each participant $k \in [K]$ holds a private dataset $\mathcal{D}_k$, and the goal is to minimize the following objective:
\begin{equation} \label{eq_PFI_global_obj}
\min_{\Omega} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} p_k F_k(w_k),
\end{equation}
where $p_k$ represents the relative data contribution of participant $k$, typically defined as $p_k = \frac{n_k}{\sum_{k=1}^{K} n_k}$ with $n_k$ being the number of data samples at participant $k$, and $F_k(\omega_k)$ denotes the local loss function evaluated on the private dataset $\mathcal{D}_k$ using model $\omega_k$. Note that $\omega_k$ is updated at each global iteration according to the aggregation rule $\omega_k = \sum_{k=1}^K p_k \omega_k$. This objective enables each participant to benefit from collaborative training within the federation while tailoring the model to its own data distribution, thereby enhancing personalized intelligence and preserving data privacy. Nevertheless, achieving PFI still faces several challenges that may hinder its effectiveness in enabling artificial personalized intelligence. In the following sections, we first explore emerging opportunities for advancing the development and practical adoption of PFI, and then discuss the key challenges and future directions towards its successful implementation.



\section{Opportunities for Personalized Federated Intelligence}
\label{sec_opportunity_PFI}

\subsection{Towards RAG-based Personalized Federated Intelligence}
Recent advancements in FMs and LLMs have demonstrated significant progress across diverse domains~\cite{guan2024federated, 9415623}. However, these models often fall short in adapting to user-specific contexts due to the limitations of static model parameters and centralized learning paradigms~\cite{bommasani2021opportunities,zhou2024comprehensive}. In the landscape of PFI, the integration of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)~\cite{lewis2020retrieval,izacard2022few} introduces a promising direction by enabling more context-aware and adaptable generation through dynamic information retrieval. When coupled with federated optimization strategies\cite{Federated_semantic}, RAG becomes a powerful tool for personalized and privacy-preserving intelligence. This section explores the role of RAG in PFI by examining its foundations, architectural considerations, privacy mechanisms, communication efficiency, and integration with FL frameworks.

\subsubsection{Fundamentals of Retrieval-Augmented Generation}
RAG~\cite{lewis2020retrieval} is a hybrid framework that integrates neural retrieval with conditional language generation. Different from standard language models that rely solely on internal parameters, RAG augments generation by retrieving relevant external documents at inference time and using them as contextual evidence for the generator. The framework consists of two core components: a neural retriever, denoted by \( \mathcal{R}_\theta \), and a generator, denoted by \( \mathcal{G}_\phi \). The retriever is typically implemented as a dense bi-encoder such as dense passage retrieval (DPR)~\cite{karpukhin2020dense}, where both the input query and the documents are encoded into a shared embedding space. The retriever score between a query \( q \) and a document \( d \) is computed as follows:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_\theta(q, d) = \text{sim}(E_q(q), E_d(d)) = E_q(q)^\top E_d(d),
\end{equation}
where \( E_q(\cdot) \) and \( E_d(\cdot) \) denote the query and document encoders, respectively, and \( \text{sim}(\cdot) \) represents dot product similarity. The retriever selects the top-\( k \) documents \( \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_k\} \) with the highest scores for a given input.


Once the relevant documents are retrieved, the generator \( \mathcal{G}_\phi \), typically instantiated as a conditional encoder-decoder model such as BART~\cite{lewis2020bart} or T5~\cite{raffel2020exploring}, produces the final output sequence \( y \). The output is conditioned on the original input \( x \) and the retrieved documents. To aggregate evidence from multiple documents, the likelihood of generating the output is computed by marginalizing over the retrieved contexts as follows:
\begin{equation}
P(y|x) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} P(y|x, d_i) P(d_i|x),
\end{equation}
where \( P(y|x, d_i) \) denotes the probability of generating \( y \) given the input \( x \) and retrieved document \( d_i \), and \( P(d_i|x) \) represents the probability of retrieving \( d_i \) based on \( x \), typically derived from the retriever's similarity scores after normalization. The training objective of RAG is to maximize the likelihood of the correct output sequence by minimizing the negative log-likelihood over the retrieval space:
% \vspace{-2mm}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{RAG}} = -\log \sum_{i=1}^{k} P(y|x, d_i) P(d_i|x),
\end{equation}
where \( \mathcal{L}_{\text{RAG}} \) denotes the loss function optimized during training. The retrieval probability \( P(d_i|x) \) can be interpreted as a soft attention weight over the retrieved documents, allowing the generator to synthesize information into coherent and factual outputs.

This architecture effectively reduces hallucination in language generation~\cite{shuster2022language}, enhances factual grounding, and supports scalable open-domain question answering. Moreover, its modular design is well-suited to federated environments, where clients maintain private and non-overlapping document collections. In such settings, the retriever and generator can be collaboratively trained using FL strategies while keeping data local~\cite{yasas2022retrieval}. On the other hand, RAG has inspired numerous extensions aimed at improving retrieval quality and generation fidelity. Notable variants include Fusion-in-Decoder~\cite{izacard2020leveraging}, which concatenates all top-\( k \) documents and jointly feeds them into the generator, and ColBERT-RAG~\cite{santhanam2022colbertv2}, which applies late-interaction retrieval for fine-grained semantic matching. In decentralized environments, federated variants such as FedRAG~\cite{yasas2022retrieval} enable privacy-preserving training of both retrieval and generation components across distributed clients, leveraging local knowledge without requiring raw data exchange.

