\subsubsection{Design Space for Retrieval-Augmented Personalized Federated Models}
Building on the foundational principles of RAG outlined above, this subsection explores how retrieval-augmented mechanisms can be tailored for use within PFI. In this setting, the unique demands of personalization, privacy preservation, communication efficiency, and cross-device generalization create a need for novel deployment strategies that extend beyond traditional monolithic fine-tuning. RAG is particularly attractive in federated environments because it decouples knowledge retrieval from generation. This enables edge clients to benefit from evolving or personalized corpora without requiring full model retraining or exposing raw data. Moreover, the retriever and generator modules in RAG can be selectively adapted to balance trade-offs between computational cost, privacy guarantees, and user-specific relevance. To structure this design space, we decompose RAG-based FFM systems into two complementary pillars: {Client-side~Personalization} and {Server-side~Knowledge-Augmented~Optimization}. The former focuses on on-device strategies that preserve user privacy while delivering contextual intelligence, while the latter describes server- or edge-tier strategies to enhance global robustness, generalization, and fairness. Below, we detail the representative techniques and their design motivations under each axis. Figure~\ref{fig:RAG_FL} provides an illustration of the key components and
strategies involved.

\paragraph{Client-side~Personalization}
\textbf{\\ Prompt Conditioning Using RAG.}
In real-world federated environments, users often require responses tailored to their specific domains, such as medical professionals needing clinical terminology, legal experts querying statutes, or individuals referencing personal data. However, fine-tuning the entire FM on each client is impractical due to its large size, the limited compute capacity of edge devices, and privacy risks associated with transmitting model updates. Prompt conditioning offers an efficient and privacy-preserving solution. Rather than updating the full model, each client learns a compact set of tunable embeddings, known as {soft prompts}, which are prepended to input queries. These prompts act as domain-specific control signals that guide the model’s behavior without altering its internal weights~\cite{lester2021power}. Typically containing only a few hundred parameters, soft prompts can be trained locally with minimal resources and shared using standard federated learning protocols~\cite{zhao2023fedprompt,li2024global}, enabling collaborative adaptation without exposing raw data.

In RAG, prompt conditioning becomes even more powerful. A RAG system consists of a retriever that selects relevant documents and a generator that synthesizes responses. Unlike standard language models, where prompts only guide generation, in RAG, they influence both document retrieval and response formulation. For example, a clinician’s prompt may steer the retriever toward medical literature and the generator toward precise clinical language, while a legal expert’s prompt may emphasize legal texts and structured argumentation. This dual influence is critical for FFMs, which aim to serve users across diverse domains using a shared and scalable backbone. With the retriever and generator fixed globally, soft prompts enable fine-grained client-side personalization without modifying or transmitting the full model. Additionally, prompts can be aggregated at the server to improve generalization. For instance, prompts from different hospitals can be clustered to create domain-level prompts, supporting broader knowledge sharing across similar clients without requiring raw data exchange. Prompt conditioning also supports continual personalization. As user needs evolve, prompts can be updated incrementally without retraining the model, aligning with FFM objectives of adaptability, privacy, and efficiency. Empirical studies show that prompt-conditioned RAG reduces hallucination, improves factual grounding, and enhances domain-specific accuracy~\cite{zhao2023fedprompt}. Compared to traditional fine-tuning, it offers a lightweight, federated, and scalable path to personalized intelligence.

\textbf{Local Knowledge Indexing for On-Device Personalization.} A central challenge in deploying FFMs lies in achieving user-level personalization while safeguarding privacy. Although FMs offer strong generalization, they often falter in highly contextual settings such as personalized document understanding, niche domain tasks, or individual knowledge retrieval, where access to user-specific information is essential~\cite{guan2024federated,9415623}. Traditional cloud-based solutions that require uploading user data for fine-tuning or indexing are incompatible with privacy regulations and communication constraints. To overcome this, \textit{local knowledge indexing} provides a promising alternative that adheres to the \textit{model to data} principle~\cite{wang2021fieldguide}, where computation occurs locally at the data source.

In RAG frameworks~\cite{lewis2020retrieval}, local indexing enables each client to build and maintain a private, queryable repository of documents, including emails, notes, and clinical records. These documents are embedded into vector representations using a local encoder and stored on devices, optionally within a Trusted Execution Environment (TEE) to ensure secure storage and inference~\cite{hu2023federated}. During inference, the retriever performs a similarity search over this private index to retrieve the top-$k$ relevant documents, which are then passed to a shared global generator model (e.g., BART~\cite{lewis2020bart} or T5~\cite{raffel2020exploring}) to synthesize personalized responses.

Recent systems such as ERAGent~\cite{shi2024eragent} demonstrate how retrieval-based personalization using local repositories can significantly improve task relevance and user satisfaction. Additionally, modular toolkits such as LlamaIndex and LangChain~\cite{llamaindex2023, langchain2023} have gained popularity for enabling lightweight, privacy-preserving retrieval pipelines that operate entirely on-device. Frameworks like FedRAG~\cite{yasas2022retrieval}, C-FedRAG~\cite{addison2024cfedrag}, and FRAG~\cite{zhao2024frag} further extend this paradigm by coordinating local retrievers and shared generators in a federated manner. For instance, C-FedRAG employs confidential computing to preserve context confidentiality while using federated optimization algorithms such as FedAvg~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} or FedProx~\cite{li2020federated} to maintain coherence across clients without sacrificing local relevance. Altogether, local knowledge indexing enhances the personalization capabilities of FFMs by anchoring responses in user-owned data, enabling privacy-preserving, adaptive, and domain-specific intelligence. When integrated with federated RAG workflows, it becomes a cornerstone technology for building practical and trustworthy personalized AI systems.

\textbf{Retriever Adaptation via LoRA or Adapters.} In federated settings, users often operate across heterogeneous domains such as healthcare, law, or education, which can lead to retrieval mismatches caused by variations in vocabulary, semantics, and document structures~\cite{guan2024federated, wang2021fieldguide}. While prompt tuning provides a lightweight mechanism to steer model behavior, it is often insufficient to address domain-specific retrieval errors, particularly when the underlying embedding space of the retriever is misaligned with the user's corpus. To address this, clients can locally fine-tune only the \textit{retriever} component of the RAG pipeline using parameter-efficient methods such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)~\cite{hu2022lora} and adapter modules. These techniques introduce small, trainable layers into pre-trained retrievers without modifying the backbone model. Because the update size remains minimal, typically under 1MB, retriever adaptations can be securely aggregated using FL protocols like FedAvg~\cite{mcmahan2017communication} or secure summation~\cite{bonawitz2017practical}, enabling scalable personalization without compromising privacy or bandwidth efficiency.

This localized retriever tuning enhances the relevance of retrieved documents while keeping the global generator unchanged, thus maintaining inference speed and memory efficiency, which are critical requirements for edge deployments~\cite{stock2020and, jiao2020tinybert}. Wang et al.~\cite{wang2024flora} demonstrate that LoRA-based retriever adaptation boosts recall by 2 to 4 times on previously unseen biomedical datasets, highlighting its effectiveness in domain generalization. Moreover, this strategy aligns with privacy-preserving design principles. Because only compressed retriever weights are shared, and not raw data or full gradients, user-specific adaptation becomes feasible without violating data protection constraints~\cite{natarajan2023chexmix}. The modularity of this setup also allows asynchronous or partial retriever updates across clients~\cite{nguyen2022federatedbuff, reisizadeh2020fedpaq}, making it suitable for deployment in low-resource or heterogeneous federated environments. In summary, retriever adaptation via LoRA or adapter modules empowers clients to improve retrieval accuracy in a lightweight, private, and communication-efficient manner, enabling scalable deployment of personalized FFMs across diverse domains.

\paragraph{Server-side Knowledge-Augmented Optimization}
While client-side personalization enables tailored responses and privacy-preserving adaptation, global coordination remains essential to ensure fairness, generalization, and robustness across diverse users. In FFMs, the server or regional edge tier is responsible not only for aggregating updates but also for enriching model capabilities through shared knowledge. By leveraging RAG at the server level, we can design intelligent orchestration strategies that optimize training dynamics and enhance sample efficiency without compromising user data.

\textbf{Retrieval-based Task Matching for Curriculum-Aligned Personalization.} Another central challenge in FL lies in reconciling statistical heterogeneity with efficient global training. In real-world deployments, clients often vary significantly in domain, task complexity, and data availability, ranging from richly annotated clinical corpora to sparse educational notes or niche financial records. To personalize global learning signals without accessing raw client data, retrieval-based task matching offers a powerful server-side coordination strategy that enhances contextual alignment during model aggregation.
In this approach, the server maintains a broad, heterogeneous corpus of publicly available or pseudo-public data, such as open domain texts, instructional datasets, or synthetic knowledge bases~\cite{guo2021fedmatch, caldas2018leaf}. For every incoming client gradient or model update, a lightweight retrieval mechanism identifies semantically related examples from the server-side corpus that reflect the client’s domain, task type, or current learning difficulty~\cite{sun2024curriculum}. These retrieved examples are used to augment the next round of training through curriculum-style updates, such as batch reweighting, importance sampling, or contrastive construction~\cite{wang2021adaptive, hsu2020fedeval}.


This design introduces a form of implicit personalization by ensuring that each client receives globally aggregated updates that are contextually relevant to its local domain. For example, if a client exhibits difficulty in a biomedical classification task, the server retrieves and integrates related medical samples to reinforce learning during the global update. This targeted augmentation reduces the mismatch between global knowledge and client needs, accelerates convergence, and mitigates catastrophic forgetting in non-IID settings. By adapting RAG principles to guide what content is emphasized during aggregation, this method enhances fairness and sample efficiency across diverse clients. Importantly, since task matching operates over abstract representations (e.g., gradients or embeddings) rather than raw data, it maintains strong privacy guarantees~\cite{guo2021fedmatch, caldas2018leaf}. Moreover, the mechanism is especially valuable in open-ended deployments where new clients with previously unseen domains continuously join the federation. In such cases, retrieval-based task matching serves as a lightweight yet effective personalization mechanism, which offers adaptive, domain-aware support without requiring direct fine-tuning or explicit supervision.

\textbf{Cluster-wise Knowledge Sharing for Domain-Aligned Personalization.} In FL, personalization frequently conflicts with global generalization due to statistical heterogeneity across clients. Users may belong to vastly different application domains—such as clinical diagnostics \cite{health}, legal document processing, wireless environments \cite{raha2025security,jin2020novel} or educational tutoring, where a single global model often fails to generalize effectively. Cluster-wise knowledge sharing offers a middle ground by grouping clients into semantically coherent clusters based on corpus-level similarity, such as cosine distance over TF-IDF vectors or latent embeddings~\cite{duan2020fedgroup, vahidian2023pacfl}.

Each cluster maintains a domain-specific\cite{raha2024boosting} RAG sub-model that comprises a retriever and an adapter module. The retriever is fine-tuned to retrieve documents relevant to the cluster’s specific domain, while the adapter provides lightweight personalization over a shared generator. This design allows intra-cluster model updates to remain focused and semantically aligned. Standard aggregation algorithms such as FedAvg or FedProx~\cite{li2020federated} are used within each cluster to synchronize the sub-models. Importantly, cross-cluster communication is not performed through raw gradients or sensitive documents, but through abstracted representations such as distilled summaries or adapter embeddings, preserving privacy and reducing communication overhead~\cite{duan2020fedgroup}.

The RAG framework is particularly valuable in this clustered setting because it decouples knowledge retrieval from language generation. While the generator remains globally shared across all clusters, each cluster-specific retriever ensures that input queries are grounded in domain-relevant knowledge. For instance, the retriever in a biomedical cluster retrieves clinical notes or research abstracts, while the one in a legal cluster fetches case law or statutes. This modularity enables domain-aligned personalization without incurring the computational cost or privacy risk of full-model fine-tuning. Additionally, RAG’s retrieval-based conditioning improves factual grounding, reduces hallucinations, and enables continual adaptation as the client corpus evolves.

Empirical studies support this hybrid strategy. For example, FedGroup~\cite{duan2020fedgroup} clusters clients based on update similarity and achieves improved accuracy and convergence, while PACFL~\cite{vahidian2023pacfl} forms clusters based on principal angles between data subspaces to ensure distributional similarity. Extending these frameworks with RAG further enhances their capability by aligning both the data distribution and the contextual retrieval pipeline. In summary, cluster-wise knowledge sharing with RAG enables federated systems to provide domain-specific personalization at scale. By tuning the retriever within each cluster while retaining a globally shared generator, it balances specialization and generalization. This architecture aligns well with the goals of PFI, where personalization, privacy, and communication efficiency must coexist in decentralized and heterogeneous environments.

