\subsection{Towards Trustworthy Personalized Federated Intelligence}
Although PFI holds great promise in enabling intelligent, privacy-preserving services across distributed environments, building trustworthy PFI systems remains a critical challenge. Without trustworthy mechanisms, end users may be reluctant to adopt or rely on PFI solutions, particularly when sensitive personal or corporate data and high-stakes decisions are involved. Therefore, fostering trust in PFI is essential to ensure its practical feasibility, societal acceptance, and long-term sustainability. This section explores the evolving landscape of trustworthy PFI, beginning with fairness considerations, followed by advancements in interpretable capabilities and strategies to mitigate hallucinations and enhance robustness against various attacks, all aimed at ensuring fairness, explainability, and reliability in PFI systems. Figure~\ref{fig:trustworthy_FL} provides an illustration of the key components and strategies involved.


\subsubsection{Mitigating Bias and Ensuring Fairness}
Fairness in FMs refers to the model's ability to avoid discriminating against individuals or groups within society~\cite{kaur2022trustworthy_survey}. Since FMs are often trained on diverse datasets collected from numerous clients, they are susceptible to inheriting and amplifying biases present in the data, which can result in unfair or discriminatory outcomes. To mitigate such issues, we explore three complementary strategies to enhance fairness in PFI as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:mitigating_bias_unfairness}: model-level processing, client-side processing, and server-side processing.

