\section{Challenges and Future Directions}
\label{sec_challenges_future}
Although FL offers a privacy-preserving paradigm for collaboratively training FMs, its direct application to such models still faces several challenges, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:challenge_PFI}. These include heterogeneity in domains, models, and system resources, communication overhead challenges, and privacy and security issues, all of which can hinder practical deployment in real-world scenarios. Understanding and mitigating these challenges is essential for advancing PFI systems in real-world scenarios. In the following section, we discuss these challenges and then highlight promising future research directions, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:future_PFI}. These include integrating the "user-agent-world" interaction ecosystem into PFI systems to enhance autonomy and adaptability, leveraging quantum computing to enable next-generation PFI, and pursuing sustainable, green PFI solutions to further advance the field.


\subsection{Challenges in PFI}


\subsubsection{Heterogeneity in Domains, Models and System Resources}

Integrating FMs into FL systems requires coordination among a wide range of participants, each with distinct characteristics. This inherent diversity introduces various forms of heterogeneity, which can significantly affect training efficiency, convergence speed, and the overall generalization performance of the resulting models~\cite{qiao2024fedccl,qiaoICC_2024knowledge}. Given the large-scale and resource-intensive nature of FMs, addressing such heterogeneity is particularly crucial in the context of PFI to ensure robust and scalable performance across participants. We categorize the heterogeneity in PFI into three major dimensions: domain heterogeneity, model heterogeneity, and system heterogeneity. we delve into each type in detail, analyzing their specific challenges and discussing potential strategies to mitigate their impact within PFI systems.

\textbf{Domain Heterogeneity.} This concept, first introduced in~\cite{qiao2024fedccl}, can be typically categorized into intra-domain and inter-domain types. Intra-domain heterogeneity can be further divided into balanced and imbalanced categories. Balanced intra-domain heterogeneity refers to samples drawn from the same domain with equal quantities but differing label distributions, while imbalanced intra-domain heterogeneity involves samples with varying quantities and label shifts within the same domain. Similarly, inter-domain heterogeneity is categorized into balanced and imbalanced types. Balanced inter-domain heterogeneity refers to samples drawn from different domains with equal quantities, while imbalanced inter-domain heterogeneity involves samples with varying quantities across domains. All of these pose significant challenges to FL, potentially hindering the convergence rate and efficiency of the model training process~\cite{mcmahan2017communication,li2021fedbn,le2023federated}. Figure~\ref{fig:doamin_challenges} illustrates these challenges for better clarity. A mainstream of traditional FL methods focuses on incorporating a regularization term to align local updates with the global model~\cite{liu2022privacy,qiao2024fedccl}. This approach can also be extended to PFI scenarios, leading to the following objective formulation:
\begin{equation}
\min_{\Omega} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \left( p_k F_k(w_k) + \mu \|w_k - w_g\|^2 \right),
\end{equation} 
where $\Omega = \{ \omega_1, \omega_2, ..., \omega_K \}$ denotes a set of personalized model parameters for all clients, $w_k$ denotes the personalized model for client $k$, $w_g$ is the global reference model, and $\mu$ is a regularization strength that encourages similarity between local and global models. This formulation is based on the intuition that the global model can serve as a relatively less biased reference to guide local updates, promoting both personalization and consistency across clients. Nevertheless, more advanced strategies, such as adaptive aggregation methods~\cite{wang2024aggregation}, domain-specific personalization techniques~\cite{sun2021partialfed}, and robust optimization frameworks~\cite{fang2022robust}, can be developed to further improve model performance and generalization.

\textbf{Model Heterogeneity.} In PFI scenarios, participating clients or organizations may adopt heterogeneous models due to differences in architecture design, training strategies, and data preprocessing pipelines~\cite{alam2022fedrolex,qiao2023cdfed,li2021model}. For example, some clients may deploy large transformer-based architectures such as ViT~\cite{naseer2021intriguing} or BERT~\cite{sun2019videobert}, while others rely on more lightweight models like MobileBERT~\cite{sun2020mobilebert}, Swin-Transformer~\cite{10423076}, TinyLLaMA~\cite{zhang2024tinyllama}, or Nano~\cite{team2023gemini} to accommodate computational or memory constraints. From a training perspective, certain participants might train models from scratch, whereas others may fine-tune pre-trained models. Similarly, in terms of data preprocessing, some clients may standardize or normalize their input data, while others might skip such steps entirely. These variations lead to model heterogeneity across clients, resulting in inconsistent feature representations and making it difficult to perform direct model aggregation, ultimately degrading the overall performance of the federated training process. To address this issue, one promising direction is to adopt knowledge distillation techniques, such as prototype-based methods~\cite{qiao2023boosting,qiao2023prototype,tan2022fedproto}, which extract intermediate-level feature representations and share them with the server without requiring structural alignment or model aggregation. This approach encourages consistency between local and global prototypes, and the process can be formalized as follows:
\begin{equation}
\min_{\Omega} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \left( p_k F_k(w_k) + \mu \sum_{l=1}^{L} \left\| \mathbf{c}_k^l - \mathbf{c}_g^l \right\|^2 \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{c}_k^l$ represents the local prototype (feature centroid) of class $l$ on client $k$, and $\mathbf{c}_g^l$ denotes the global prototype for class $l$. Here, $L$ is the number of classes. Note that in this approach, the local parameters are not sent to the server for aggregation. Instead, each client trains its own model while absorbing knowledge from the global prototypes to mitigate the impact of model heterogeneity. In addition, transfer learning presents another effective solution by enabling clients to adapt shared knowledge to their specific model architectures and tasks~\cite{otoum2022feasibility,saha2021federated,10755087,zhang2021survey}, offering a flexible framework that facilitates collaboration among participants with heterogeneous models while preserving both personalization and performance.

