\textbf{System Heterogeneity.} The hardware, communication capabilities, and computational resources of participating entities in PFI may differ due to variability in device types, network conditions, and resource availability, leading to system heterogeneity~\cite{li2020federated,van2009multi}. In terms of hardware, this includes variations across devices, such as smartphones, laptops, or IoT devices, each offering different processing power. For communication capabilities, this includes differences in network bandwidth and latency, with some devices having access to high-speed 5G connections~\cite{adhikary2023integrated,adhikary2023intelligent}, while others rely on slower and less stable networks, such as 4G, Wi-Fi with congestion issues, or low-power wide-area networks (LPWAN)~\cite{raza2017low,adhikary2024holographic} that prioritize coverage over speed. As for computational resources, this includes variations in factors like battery capacity and processing power, ranging from high-performance devices to resource-constrained edge devices. These heterogeneities may pose challenges such as straggler effects, where slower devices delay global model updates, inefficient resource utilization, and potential communication bottlenecks~\cite{qiao2023framework}. Therefore, the methods designed for PFI must be resilient to hardware heterogeneity and robust against device dropouts in communication networks. One promising approach is asynchronous aggregation~\cite{nguyen2022federated}, which helps mitigate the straggler effects caused by slower devices. By allowing clients to update the global model independently, without waiting for all participants to complete their computations, asynchronous aggregation ensures that the global model evolves continuously, even when some clients experience delays. The update rule for asynchronous aggregation can be expressed as follows:
\begin{equation}
w_g^{t+1} = w_g^t + \sum_{k \in S_t} p_k \left( w_k^t - w_g^t \right),
\end{equation}
where $S_t$ represents the set of participants at round $t$, and $p_k$ is the weight corresponding to client $k$. In addition, adaptive client selection~\cite{marfo2025adaptive} can further address hardware heterogeneity by dynamically choosing clients based on their available resources, such as processing power or stable network conditions, ensuring efficient model updates. Moreover, asynchronous tiered frameworks~\cite{chai2021fedat} can integrate synchronous training within homogeneous tiers and asynchronous training across heterogeneous tiers, which helps mitigate straggler effects, accelerate convergence, and sustain high model performance under diverse system configurations.




\subsubsection{Communication Overhead Challenges}
In FL scenarios, each participant must engage in collaborative training and coordinate with the central server through the exchange of model parameters, all while keeping local data on-device~\cite{mcmahan2017communication}. This frequent communication can introduce significant overhead, especially in bandwidth-constrained environments or when dealing with large-scale models. 

\textbf{Model Size Constraints.} A major challenge arises from the massive size of modern foundation models. For example, deploying LLMs such as LLaMA-2~\cite{touvron2023llama} within an FL framework can be extremely communication-intensive. LLaMA-2 7B contains over 7 billion parameters, and transferring its full-precision model (32-bit floats) would require approximately 28 GB per communication round. In a typical FL setting involving hundreds of clients, this would result in terabytes of data transfer per round, placing an overwhelming burden on network bandwidth and device energy consumption, particularly for edge devices with limited connectivity~\cite{zhang2019deep,10004947,jin2025resilient}. 

\textbf{System and Deployment Constraints.} In real-world deployments such as cross-institutional medical systems, where devices are often constrained by private networks or only allowed to communicate during predefined intervals, full model synchronization becomes impractical~\cite{yin2024federated}. These constraints can lead to significant overhead, ultimately hindering the training process~\cite{mcmahan2017communication,konevcny2016federated}. 

\textbf{Mitigation Strategies.} To address these challenges, a variety of techniques have been proposed to enhance communication efficiency in PFI. These include model compression methods such as pruning~\cite{jiang2022model}, quantization~\cite{shlezinger2020uveqfed}, low-rank decomposition~\cite{wu2024decoupling}, and knowledge distillation~\cite{qiao2023knowledge}, all of which aim to reduce the size of transmitted models while preserving performance. Additionally, PEFT techniques, such as prompt tuning~\cite{zhao2023fedprompt}, adapter tuning~\cite{chen2024feddat}, and LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, enable clients to share and update only a small subset of parameters. Sparse update strategies~\cite{rothchild2020fetchsgd} further reduce communication by transmitting only significant gradients or parameter changes. Moreover, approaches like periodic aggregation\cite{hu2023scheduling} and adaptive update schemes~\cite{wu2023faster}, along with strategies such as client selection tailored to address processing system heterogeneity, dynamically adjust communication frequency based on network conditions and model convergence progress, collectively contribute to the mitigation of communication overhead.

