\subsection{Future Directions for PFI}

\subsubsection{Meta-PFI}
The integration of autonomous capabilities into PFI systems offers a transformative opportunity to enhance the adaptability and autonomy of federated intelligence. We define this paradigm shift as meta-personalized federated intelligence (Meta-PFI), which marks a significant advancement in the flexibility and capabilities of personalized federated foundation models. At its core, Meta-PFI operates within a "user-agent-world" interaction ecosystem, where users (whether human or avatar) provide real-time behavioral data and feedback, agents (embodied federated AI agents) autonomously adjust their strategies based on this input or their perception of the world, and the world (comprising both real and virtual environments) shapes and influences the learning process. We discuss how several promising key technologies can enable the realization of Meta-PFI: 1) virtual technologies, which enhance interaction and contextual awareness in both digital and physical environments; 2) self-learning techniques, which empower the system to continuously learn and optimize based on dynamic user inputs and interactions; and 3) embodied federated AI agents, which combine the capabilities of FL and embodied AI agents to enable adaptive, personalized behavior across diverse environments.


\textbf{Virtual Technologies for Meta-PFI.} 
Virtual technologies~\cite{alhakamy2024extended} such as VR, AR, MR, XR, and digital twins play a pivotal role in enabling the user-agent-world ecosystem at the core of Meta-PFI. These technologies create immersive, context-rich environments that bridge the physical and digital environment, allowing agents to more effectively perceive, interpret, and respond to users’ personalized needs. For instance, AR and MR systems can anchor virtual agents or objects in real-world settings, facilitating seamless multimodal interactions. A notable example is Apple Vision Pro~\cite{cheng2024first}, an MR headset that represents a breakthrough in spatial computing. It seamlessly blends digital content with the physical environment, enabling immersive interaction, communication, and productivity through intuitive controls based on eye movement, hand gestures, and voice controls. Additionally, digital twin systems create virtual replicas of users and their environments, enabling virtual agents to better understand user behaviors and environmental dynamics and make more informed decisions. For example, Gecko Robotics and L3Harris Technologies~\cite{Gecko2025Robotics} have developed an XR-powered digital twin solution for aircraft maintenance that generates detailed 3D models from high-resolution surface imagery, enabling efficient and remote inspection and sustainment operations. Through these virtual infrastructures, Meta-PFI systems can continuously gather contextual cues from user instructions and environments, simulate user-agent interactions, and refine personalized behaviors in an adaptive, context-aware, and interactive manner.





\textbf{Self-Learning for Meta-PFI.} 
In addition to offering diverse experiences and new cognitive perspectives through virtual technologies, the self-learning capability in Meta-PFI is central to its effectiveness. It enables agents to dynamically adapt and refine their strategies in response to changing environments and evolving user preferences, without requiring continuous human intervention. This capability is typically supported by techniques such as reinforcement learning~\cite{shao2018visual}, meta-learning~\cite{fallah2020personalized}, continual learning~\cite{aggarwal2023chameleon}, and online adaptation~\cite{poggi2024federated}. Specifically, RL is a learning paradigm in which agents acquire optimal decision-making policies by interacting with an environment through trial and error~\cite{shao2018visual}. Unlike supervised learning, RL does not rely on labeled input-output pairs or explicit correction signals for suboptimal actions. Instead, agents learn to make sequential decisions by receiving feedback in the form of rewards. One of the core challenges in RL is to balance exploration, which involves seeking new strategies or unfamiliar states, with exploitation, which focuses on leveraging existing knowledge to maximize cumulative rewards over time. In the context of Meta-PFI, RL still plays a vital role in empowering agents with autonomous self-learning capabilities through continuous interaction with users and the world. For instance, value-based methods such as DQN~\cite{zhou2024game} can help agents on mobile or wearable devices learn efficient user-specific policies (e.g., for personalized content recommendation or battery-aware task scheduling) through interactions with users and their environments. Policy-based methods, such as DPG~\cite{shi2023optimal}, allow these agents to directly optimize for personalized user experiences, such as gesture recognition or adaptive content recommendations, without relying on a predefined value function. Moreover, actor–critic methods~\cite{tang2025deep} such as A2C, A3C, TD3, and SAC are promising in managing both shared global policies and local personalization. For example, in a cross-silo setting, a network of hospitals deploying a federated medical assistant agent could leverage A3C~\cite{yan2024multi} to collaboratively train a shared global model while capturing the unique interaction patterns specific to each institution. The asynchronous nature of A3C also accelerates training by allowing multiple agents to collect data in parallel, with each interacting independently within its own environment. In addition to RL, other deep self-learning strategies, such as meta-learning~\cite{fallah2020personalized} and continual learning~\cite{li2023personalized}, can further enhance the autonomy and adaptability of Meta-PFI. For example, meta-learning, such as MAML~\cite{collins2020task}, enables agents to quickly adapt to new tasks or users with minimal data by "learning how to learn", which involves leveraging prior knowledge and experience to guide the acquisition of new tasks and generalize learning strategies across diverse scenarios. Continual learning, on the other hand, helps models retain knowledge across evolving user interactions, thereby mitigating the risk of catastrophic forgetting. Together, these approaches are expected to empower Meta-PFI to sustain long-term personalization while remaining responsive to real-time behavioral signals, environmental dynamics, and shifting user intents.


\textbf{Embodied Federated AI Agents for Meta-PFI.} To realize the Meta-PFI paradigm in the future, it is essential to move beyond embodied AI agents~\cite{durante2024agent} operating in centralized environments and instead develop embodied federated AI agents capable of functioning in distributed environments. We envision that the embodied federated AI agents will not only possess physical embodiments to support interaction and perception but also be capable of collaborating with other agents to accomplish tasks in a privacy-preserving manner. These agents are capable of operating in both physical and digital spaces, leveraging FL to collaborate without sharing raw data, while continuously adapting to user preferences and environmental changes. In addition to being able to collaborate with other agents in a privacy-preserving manner, the federated AI agents embodied in Meta-PFI also have the ability to perform reasoning, maintain long-term memory, and leverage external tools such as RAG~\cite{lewis2020retrieval} to adapt their behavior based on the situation. A core component of such agents is FMs, such as LLMs, which serve as their central reasoning engine or "brain". To further enhance this reasoning capability, the DeepSeek-R1 models~\cite{guo2025deepseek} have recently demonstrated that pure RL, without relying on human or AI feedback, can effectively improve the reasoning performance of LLMs. This highlights a promising direction for advancing the development of embodied federated AI agents. Overall, we believe that the integration of virtual technologies, self-learning capabilities, and the rapid development of embodied AI agents will collectively accelerate the realization of Meta-PFI systems, enabling more personalized, adaptive, and privacy-preserving intelligent services across diverse environments.




\subsubsection{Quantum-Enabled PFI}
As the scale and complexity of PFI continue to grow, classical computing approaches may struggle to meet the demands of real-time personalization, massive-scale model training, and ultra-secure data handling. Quantum computing~\cite{mathur2025federated} offers a promising future direction for enabling next-generation PFI systems. By leveraging quantum parallelism and entanglement, quantum-enabled PFI could accelerate federated model optimization, enhance privacy-preserving mechanisms through quantum cryptography, and enable fundamentally more efficient solutions to complex non-convex optimization problems encountered in federated learning. Quantum machine learning algorithms~\cite{caro2022generalization} could allow faster convergence and better generalization when training personalized models across heterogeneous devices. Furthermore, quantum-enhanced federated systems can facilitate stronger privacy guarantees using quantum key distribution protocols~\cite{padamvathi2016quantum}, ensuring secure model updates even under powerful adversaries. Despite its enormous potential, quantum-enabled PFI faces significant challenges, including the need for hybrid classical-quantum frameworks, managing quantum decoherence, and adapting current federated architectures to quantum computing paradigms. Nevertheless, as quantum technologies mature, integrating quantum computing into PFI is expected to open new frontiers in scalable, personalized, and highly secure intelligent networks.

\textbf{Quantum Security for PFI.}
The secure quantum-based adder design can be effectively utilized as an extraordinary hardware component within PFI systems to significantly enhance their security posture \cite{ain2025secure}. By embedding such a quantum-secure circuit into PFI architectures, critical user data, model parameters, and personalization layers can be robustly shielded from side-channel physical attacks, which exploit inadvertent information leakage during computation and communication processes. Common side-channel threats, such as power analysis, electromagnetic emissions, and timing attacks, pose major risks to the confidentiality and integrity of PFI environments, where personal and sensitive information is distributed across a large number of heterogeneous clients. Integrating specialized quantum-enabled circuits~\cite{ju2024quantum} provides a dedicated hardware-based security layer, complementing existing algorithmic defenses like differential privacy, secure aggregation, and federated personalization techniques. This hybrid security architecture ensures that even if physical vulnerabilities are targeted, the overall PFI system maintains resilience, preserving both the privacy of user models and the trustworthiness of the FL process across training and inference phases.

 \textbf{Quantum-Driven Aggregation for PFI.}
To address vulnerabilities associated with centralized aggregation in PFI, it is crucial to rethink server and client selection strategies in a quantum-aware context \cite{mathur2025federated}. A promising direction involves the development of a quantum-probabilistic framework~\cite{chowdhury2019probabilistic} that leverages quantum randomness and quantum interference to enable secure, unpredictable, and highly dynamic aggregation processes. In this approach, quantum circuits are utilized to generate cryptographically secure random selection vectors, allowing for entropy-driven, decentralized selection of multiple servers and clients for each aggregation round. This quantum-enhanced mechanism not only mitigates risks such as server collusion and biased client sampling but also strengthens the overall resilience, scalability, and security of the PFI framework by removing the single point of failure inherent in traditional centralized aggregation.

\textbf{Quantum Wireless Networks for PFI.}
A promising future research direction for PFI is the integration of quantum wireless networks~\cite{wang2022quantum} to enable ultra-secure and low-latency communication across distributed clients and servers. Quantum wireless networks, leveraging technologies such as entanglement distribution and quantum repeaters, can provide end-to-end security guarantees against both classical and quantum-enabled threats. In the context of PFI, these networks would allow the transmission of personalized model updates and aggregation information with unconditional security, even over mobile and dynamic environments. Future research could explore how quantum entanglement-based synchronization and quantum channel adaptation can optimize the reliability, scalability, and responsiveness of federated learning in wireless settings. Additionally, integrating quantum wireless communication with edge intelligence and context-aware networking would significantly enhance the robustness and efficiency of PFI, paving the way for next-generation AI systems capable of operating securely and seamlessly in 6G and beyond ecosystems. There is a growing body of research on wireless networks that focuses on optimizing wireless communication, enhancing network efficiency, and improving system performance with novel ideas \cite{adhikary2024achievable, adhikary2024transfer, adhikaryage, adhikary2024location, adhikary2023artificial_noms, adhikary2023integrated, adhikary2023artificial_icoin, adhikary2023intelligent, adhikary2024power, adhikary2024holographic, rahman2025deep, 10556146}. As quantum technologies evolve and the concept of PFI continues to gain traction, the approaches discussed in these wireless network studies could be leveraged to support the integration of quantum technologies with PFI, paving the way for future advancements in secure, scalable, and efficient communication systems. In conclusion, the combination of quantum security, quantum-driven aggregation, and quantum wireless networks for PFI can create a robust, secure, and high-performance framework, enabling the next generation of intelligent, privacy-preserving communication systems.

\subsubsection{Sustainable and Green PFI}
Sustainable and green PFI represents a promising paradigm at the intersection of edge intelligence, personalization, and environmental sustainability. By enabling decentralized learning across edge devices, PFI reduces the need for extensive data transmission to central servers, thereby minimizing energy consumption and carbon footprint. This is particularly important as the scale of connected devices grows in smart cities, IoT ecosystems, and 6G networks. Sustainable PFI emphasizes not only the efficient use of computational resources but also the deployment of energy-aware machine learning models and optimization strategies that adapt to resource availability and environmental constraints. Moreover, integrating renewable energy sources and hardware-level efficiency improvements (e.g., low-power AI chips) into federated architectures can further support eco-friendly intelligence. Ultimately, green PFI fosters intelligent systems that are not only adaptive and personalized but also aligned with long-term goals of digital sustainability and climate-conscious computing. To fully harness its potential, several future research directions can be explored:

\textbf{Energy-Aware Model Optimization for PFI.}
As PFI continues to scale across heterogeneous edge devices, energy-aware model optimization becomes vital for sustainable deployment \cite{li2022energy}. Unlike traditional FL, PFI requires models tailored to individual users' data distributions, leading to increased computational demands and energy consumption during local training and updates. Future research should focus on developing lightweight and adaptive models that can dynamically adjust their complexity based on device energy budgets and user context. Building on existing methods such as model pruning, quantization, neural architecture search, and layer-wise freezing, future efforts should explore more context-aware, energy-adaptive, and personalization-preserving strategies to optimize performance under resource constraints. Moreover, energy-efficient training strategies that leverage early stopping, selective backpropagation, and conditional computation offer promising avenues to minimize unnecessary energy consumption. By incorporating energy profiling tools and optimization routines into the training workflow, next-generation PFI systems may intelligently balance personalization fidelity with energy efficiency, contributing to the realization of sustainable and environmentally conscious AI at the edge.




\textbf{Green Communication Protocols for PFI.}
To achieve energy efficiency and sustainability in PFI, it is essential to develop green communication protocols that minimize the energy consumed during model transmission and client-server coordination \cite{liang2024communication}. Unlike conventional FL, where uniform communication strategies are applied across heterogeneous devices, PFI demands adaptive and personalized communication schemes that consider user-specific characteristics such as device capability, energy availability, and network conditions. Green communication protocols can incorporate techniques like energy-aware scheduling, dynamic compression, gradient sparsification, and client selection mechanisms that prioritize users with favorable energy and channel conditions. Furthermore, cooperative communication strategies, including device-to-device relaying and opportunistic edge caching, can significantly reduce the reliance on high-power transmissions. These energy-efficient protocols not only reduce the carbon footprint of distributed learning systems but also extend the participation lifespan of battery-constrained devices, making PFI more practical for real-world sustainable deployments.

\textbf{Green Client Scheduling for PFI.}
Another important future direction in sustainable and green PFI lies in the development of eco-aware client selection and scheduling mechanisms \cite{chen2024towards}. In real-world federated settings, participating client devices, such as smartphones, edge servers, or IoT sensors exhibit heterogeneity not only in terms of data quality and computational capacity but also in energy availability and carbon footprint. Traditional client selection schemes prioritize accuracy or latency, often ignoring the energy cost and environmental impact of engaging certain devices. Therefore, it is crucial to design intelligent selection frameworks that incorporate sustainability criteria into the client participation process.
This can be achieved by integrating multi-objective optimization techniques that simultaneously consider personalization accuracy, data heterogeneity, device energy level, and energy source (e.g., renewable vs. non-renewable). For instance, prioritizing clients powered by green energy sources (e.g., solar or wind-powered edge devices) can substantially reduce the carbon emissions associated with distributed training. Furthermore, context-aware scheduling algorithms can dynamically assign training tasks based on real-time information such as device charging state, availability of renewable energy, and user activity patterns to avoid unnecessary energy consumption during peak usage periods. In addition, eco-aware scheduling could be enhanced through RL-based strategies that learn optimal scheduling policies over time, adapting to changing energy and data distributions in the system. Incorporating predictive models that forecast green energy availability (e.g., solar panel output) can allow PFI systems to plan energy-efficient training rounds in advance. By aligning training workloads with periods of high renewable energy generation or low grid demand, these methods can improve energy efficiency while maintaining high model performance and personalization.
Ultimately, this direction supports the broader goal of enabling carbon-conscious AI at the edge, where intelligent learning systems are not only accurate and personalized but also environmentally responsible. This shift is essential to ensure that federated intelligence scales sustainably with the growing deployment of edge devices and global AI infrastructure. Overall, the integration of energy-aware model optimization, green communication protocols, and green client scheduling for PFI will not only enhance system efficiency and personalization but also contribute to a sustainable, environmentally-conscious framework for future AI deployments. The key enablers and related works are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:future_work_PFI}.




\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this survey, we provide a comprehensive overview of the emerging era of artificial personalized intelligence (API), which complements the development of artificial general intelligence (AGI), with a particular focus on the enabling technologies of personalized federated intelligence (PFI). We revisit the foundations of FL and FMs, propose unified taxonomies for both, and identify key research gaps by examining their intersection, particularly in the context of end-user personalization and privacy protection. Through the lens of PFI, we examine RAG-enhanced, efficiency-driven, and trust-oriented personalization strategies that collectively enable user-centric intelligence at the edge. We further outline a forward-looking research agenda that includes directions such as Meta-PFI, quantum-enabled learning, and sustainable intelligence, aiming to advance future PFI systems that are autonomous, scalable, and environmentally sustainable for decentralized environments. Beyond PFI, we believe that other research communities will continue to drive the development of API, opening up intriguing opportunities for future research.


