\subsection{CF-RS-Join with Filter-and-Verification Tree (FVT)}
\label{sucsec:tree based structure}

This section presents the CF-RS-Join with filter-and-verfication (FVT) (CF-RS-Join/FVT for short). We first introduce the FVT data structure and its construction process, followed by the R-S Join computation using the constructed FVT. We then explain how filtering and verification are integrated into a single stage for R-S Join, and provide a formal proof of CF-RS-Join/FVT's correctness.

\subsubsection{FVT Structure and Construction}
\label{subsubsec:AP-tree structure}
An FVT for a set collection will compress its elements and their associated sets in memory for R-S Join computation. Fig.~\ref{fig:AP-tree} depicts the FVT constructed for collection $\text{S}$, denoted as \(\texttt{FVT}_{\text{S}} = (\mathcal{E}_{\text{S}}, \mathcal{T}_{\text{S}})\), where \(\mathcal{E}_{\text{S}}\) is an element table and it is to enable fast lookup sets having a particular element in the collection. While \(\mathcal{T}_{\text{S}}\) is a tree and its node $n_{s_j}$ represent a set \(S_j in \text{S}\), where \(s_j\) is the set id of \(S_j\). Meanwhile, a node $n_{s_j}$ is represented with a 3-tuple, denoted as $n_{s_j} =((s_j,|S_j|),cld,par)$, where \(s_j\) is the set id of \(S_j\), set $S_j \in \text{S}$, $|S_j|$ is the size of the set $S_j$, $cld$ is a set of pointers pointing to a child node in tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\text{S}}\), and $par$ is a pointer pointing to the parent node in the tree. Particularly, \(cld = \phi\) means that the node has no child, and \(par = NULL\) means that the node is the root node. Meanwhile, the root node, denoted as $n_\text{root} = ((\emptyset, 0), cld, NULL)$.
% always represents an empty set?
In addition, for any node $n_{s_j}$ on a path from the root to a leaf, the larger the \(|S_j|\), the closer the node \(n_{s_j}\) is to the root. This is to guarantee nodes with bigger sets are more likely to be shared in the tree, which compresses data representation and makes the tree compact.

Figs. \ref{fig:Transpose S to S'} and \ref{fig:AP-tree} depict the construction an FVT from the sample collection of sets $\text{S}_f$ with one database scan, which includes two steps described as follows.

\noindent{\bf Step 1: Reorganize the set collection data:} A new set of \(\langle k, v\rangle\) elements is generated by the reorganization of set collection $\mathcal{S}_f$, where the key \(k\) is any \(a_i \in \mathcal{S}\) and \(\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{A}\), and value \(v = \text{seq}(a_i)\), an ordered sequence of 2-tuples: \((s_j,|S_j|)\), \(a_i \in S_j\) and \(S_j \in \mathcal{S}\). Meanwhile, the larger \(|S_j|\) is, the closer the 2-tuple is  to the left-most. For example, for an element \(a_5\) in Fig. \ref{fig:sample S}, it is in sets \(S_1\), \(S_2\), \(S_5\) and \(S_6\), then value of key \(a_5\), i.e., \(\text{seq}(a_5)\), in Fig. \ref{fig:Transpose S to S'} is an ordered sequence \(\text{seq}(a_5) = \langle (s_1,5),(s_2,5),(s_5,3),(s_6,1)\rangle\). The reorganized data of \(\text{S}_f\) is denoted as \(\text{S}'_f\).

\noindent{\bf Step 2: Construct the FVT over the reorganized data:} FVT \(\texttt{FVT}_{\mathcal{S}}\) is to be constructed over the reorganized data \(\mathcal{S}'_f\), including its element table \(\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}\) and the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\). For each entry in \(\mathcal{S}'_f\), \((a_i,\text{seq}(a_i))\), a new path, denoted as \(p(a_i)\), from the root to the leaf, \(p(a_i) = \langle n_\text{root}, n_k, n_{k+1}, \ldots, n_{k+|\text{seq}(a_i)|-1} \rangle\) will be created if it does not exist in tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\), where the 2-tuple \((s_k,|S_k|)\) of \(n_{s_k}\) is equal to the one of the first 2-tuple of \(\text{seq}(a_i)\), and the 2-tuple \((s_{k+1},|S_{k+1}|)\) of \(n_{s_{k+1}}\) is equal to the one of the second 2-tuple of \(\text{seq}(a_i)\), and so on. After the new path has been added to the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\), a new entry with a 3-tuple format will be created in the element table: \(\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}} \gets \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}} \cup \{(a_i, |\text{seq}(a_i)|, L(a_i))\}\), where $|\text{seq}(a_i)|$ is the size of $\text{seq}(a_i)$, and \(L(a_i)\) is a pointer pointing to the last node of \(p(a_i)\), i.e., the \((|\text{seq}(a_i)|-1)^{th}\) successor of \(n_{s_j}\). Particularly, if \(p(a_i)\) already exists in the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\), no new path will be created, only the corresponding entry in the element table will be added if it is not in the element table. For example, for the first entry in \(\text{S}'_f\), $\text{seq}(a_1) = \langle (s_1,5), (s_2,5), (s_3,3), (s_5,3), (s_4,2)\rangle$ in Fig. \ref{fig:Transpose S to S'}, a new path, i.e., $p(a_1) = \langle n_\text{root} , n_{s_1} , n_{s_2}, n_{s_3} , n_{s_5} , n_{s_4} \rangle$ , is added in the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\text{S}}\), as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:AP-tree}. For the third entry in \(\text{S}'_f\), $\text{seq}(a_3) = \langle (s_1,5), (s_2,5), (s_3,3), (s_5,3) \rangle$, the path \(p(a_3)\) is equal the common prefix of \(p(a_1)\) and \(p(a_3)\), no new node is needed to be added to the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\text{S}}\), only a new 3-tuple is added to the element table \(\mathcal{E}_{\text{S}}\): \((a_3, 4, L(a_3))\), and \(L(a_3)\) points to \(n_{s_5}\) in the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\text{S}}\). 

\subsubsection{The CF-RS-Join/FVT Algorithm}
\label{subsubsec:AP-treeR-SJ}
Before presenting CF-RS-Join/FVT, we first describe the length filtering strategy which enables early stop to avoid unnecessary FVT traversals. As defined in Lemma \ref{lma:len filter}~\cite{rong2017fast}, sets having low similarity in set size will have low set similarity, corresponding proof of Lemma is provided in the same paper~\cite{rong2017fast}. Length filtering strategy has been applied in many SSJs for reducing candidate set size by pruning set pairs with low similarity in set size, e.g., MetricJoin~\cite{widmoser2023metricjoin}. 

\begin{lemma}[Length Filter]
\label{lma:len filter}
    Given two sets $R_i$ and $S_j$, and a threshold $t$, if $\text{Jaccard}(R_i, S_j) \ge t$, then $\left \lceil   t\times|R_i| \right \rceil  \le |S_j| \le \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor $.
\end{lemma}

Based on the FVT constructed on \(\mathcal{S}_f\) in \S\ref{subsubsec:AP-tree structure}, i.e., \(\texttt{FVT}_{\mathcal{S}}\), let \(f_{i,j}\) denote the intersection size of \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}_f\) and \(S_j \in \mathcal{S}_f\), which is initialized to be 0. The CF-RS-Join/FVT algorithm computes the set intersection size of each set \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\) with any \(S_j \in \mathcal{S}\) by traversing \(\texttt{FVT}_{\mathcal{S}}\) from the node indexed by \(a_k \in R_i\) in the element table \(\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}\) to the root, and outputs all set pairs with similarity greater than or equal to the threshold \(t\). 

\begin{algorithm}[tb]
% \small
\footnotesize
\caption{The CF-RS-Join/FVT algorithm}
\label{alg2}
\KwIn{Two set collections $\mathcal{R}$ and $\mathcal{S}$, a threshold $t$}

\KwOut{\small $\mathcal{P} = \{ (r_i,s_j) \mid R_i \in \mathcal{R}, S_j \in \mathcal{S}, \text{sim}(R_i, S_j) \geq t\}$}

Construct $\texttt{FVT}_{\mathcal{S}} = (\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}, \mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}})$ over $\mathcal{S}$

Initialize $\mathcal{P}\gets \phi$  %最后的结果

\For{\textnormal{each} $\langle r_i,R_i\rangle \in \mathcal{R}_f$}{
$\mathcal{F} \gets \phi, \mathcal{N}\gets [\;], r_{\min}, r_{\max} \gets \lceil |R_i|\times t\rceil, \lfloor |R_i|/t \rfloor$

\For{ \textnormal{each} $a_k \in R_i$}{ %对于每个r的处理，

    $node \gets \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}_f}[a_k].L{(a_k)}$

    Append $node$ to $\mathcal{N}$
}

    Sort $\mathcal{N}$ by $\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}_f}[a_k].|\text{seq}(a_i)|$ with increasing order

    \While{$\mathcal{N} \neq \phi$}{
    $node \gets \textnormal{the last element in } \mathcal{N}$

    $support \gets 1$

    Remove $node$ \text{in} $\mathcal{N}$

        \While{ $node \neq n_\textnormal{root}$ \textnormal{and} $node.|S_j| \leq r_{\max}$} {
            \If{$node \in \mathcal{N}$}{
                $support \gets support+1$

                Remove $node$ \text{in} $\mathcal{N}$
            }

            \If{$node.|S_j| \ge r_{\min}$}{
            %进入判断语句
                \If{$f_{i,j} \notin \mathcal{F}$}{

                    $f_{i,j}\gets 0$

                    $\mathcal{F} \gets \mathcal{F} \cup \{f_{i,j}\}$
                }

                Update $f_{i,j}$ $\in$ $\mathcal{F}$ 
                with $f_{i,j} + support$
            }
            $node \gets node.par$
        }
    }

\For{\textnormal{each} $f_{i,j} \in \mathcal{F}$}{
    Calculate $\text{Jaccard}(R_i,S_j)$ by $f_{i,j}$

    \If{\textnormal{Jaccard}$(R_i,S_j)\ge t$}{
        $\mathcal{P} \gets \mathcal{P} \cup \{(r_i,s_j)\}$
    }
}
}
\end{algorithm}

For any \(a_k \in R_i\), nodes indexed via \(L(a_k) \in  \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}\) will be denoted as a set, i.e., \(\mathcal{N}\), which will be initialized to be \(\phi\) before the traversal starts from \(L(a_k)\). For each \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}_f\), \(f_{i,j}\) is accumulated by traversing the FVT to find the set \(S_j\) containing \(a_k \in R_i\). To be specific, for each \(a_k \in R_i\), we find the 3-tuple in the element table having element \(a_k\), \((a_k, |\text{seq}(a_k)|, L(a_k))\) and put the node \(n_{s_k}\) indexed by $L(a_k)$ into \(\mathcal{N}\), i.e., \(\mathcal{N} = \mathcal{N} \cup \{n_{s_k}\}\). Then the traversal starts from the first node \(n_{s_k}\) in \(\mathcal{N}\) to the root of the FVT. Let the node under visited be denoted as \(n_{s_j}\), which is initialized to be \(n_{s_k}\) at the beginning of the traversal. The traversal is described as follows, where the length filtering strategy is applied for an early stop to avoid the unnecessary tree traversal, and the pseudo code of the CF-RS-Join/FVT is described in Algorithm~\ref{alg2}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \(|S_j| < \left \lceil  t \times |R_i| \right \rceil\): $n_{s_j}$ is ignored and its parent will be visited, and \(f_{i,j}\) is untouched since the set size of $S_j$ and $R_i$ is not similar.

    \item \(\left \lceil  t \times |R_i| \right \rceil \leq |S_j| \leq \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor \): \(f_{i,j} = f_{i,j} + 1\). If \(n_{s_j}\)'s parent is not the root node, let \(n_{s_j}\) be \(n_{s_j}\)'s parent, traversal continue. Otherwise, the traversal for \(a_k\) stops and the tree traversal for the next element in \(R_i\) starts. 

    \item \(|S_j| > \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor \): The traversal stops and the tree traversal for the next element in \(R_i\) starts, since the set size of \(n_{s_j}\)'s parent is bigger than that of \(n_{s_j}\). Recall that in FVT, the closer a node is to the root, the larger the corresponding set size. 
\end{itemize}

Once a node in $\mathcal{N}$ is visited, it will be removed from $\mathcal{N}$. The similarity will be verified once \(\mathcal{N} = \phi\), then the next entry in \(\mathcal{R}_f\) will be processed until all entries have been processed. Then after the traversal of the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\), we obtain all set pairs with similarity no less than the threshold. That is, we have Lemma~\ref{lemma2}. During the traversal process described above, when the length filter is used to skip nodes whose lengths do not meet within \([\left \lceil  t \times |R_i| \right \rceil , \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor ]\), the traversal path will be shortened and we can still obtain all set pairs with similarity no less than the threshold. Then we have Theorem~\ref{theorem}.
%i.e., FVT based R-S Join, CF-RS-Join/FVT, output the exact set of set pairs satisfying the threshold. 
\begin{lemma}[Correctness of CF-RS-Join/FVT w/o Length Filter based Early Stop]
\label{lemma2}
    Given two set collections, $\mathcal{R}$ and $\mathcal{S}$, for any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\), for any $a_k \in {R_i}$, starting from $(a_k,|\text{seq}(a_k)|, L(a_k))\in \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}$, traverse from the node pointed by $L(a_k)$ to its parent, and the parent of its parent, until to the root node $n_\text{root}$ on the \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\), any \(S_j \in \mathcal{S}\) having \(a_k\) will be visited, and \(f_{i,j} = f_{i,j} + 1\) will be conducted on each visit. After the traversal is completed, we have $f_{i,j}={|R_i\cap S_j|}$ for any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\) and \(S_j \in \mathcal{S}\), and then we can have all set pairs with similarity no less than the threshold.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\) and any $S_j \in \mathcal{S}$, $f_{i,j}$ is initialized to be 0. For any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\), and for any $a_k \in R_i$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $(a_k,|\text{seq}(a_k)|, L(a_k)) \notin \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}_f}$: $\forall {s}_j\in \mathcal{S}_f$, there is $ a_k \notin S_j $, then we have $a_k \notin R_i\cap S_j$;

    \item $(a_k,|\text{seq}(a_k)|, L(a_k)) \in \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}_f}$: Traverse the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\) from the node pointed by $L(a_k)$ to its parent, and the parent of its parent, until to the root node $n_\text{root}$, where every 2-tuple $(s_j,|S_j|) \in \text{seq}(a_k)$ in $S'_f$ has been visited. For any $(s_j,|S_j|) \in \text{seq}(a_k)$, we have $a_k \in S_j$, thus $a_k \in R_i\cap S_j$. Accordingly, $f_{i,j} = f_{i,j} + 1$.
\end{itemize}
Based on the above discussion, during the traversal from the node pointed by $L(a_k)$ to the root node $n_\text{root}$ along each node's parent pointer, $f_{i,j} = f_{i,j} + 1$ will be conducted
% if and only if
iff
$a_k \in R_i\cap S_j$. Since $a_k$ is unique in the element table \(\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}\), after the traversal, $f_{i,j}$ is the number of elements shared by $R_i$ and $S_j$, that is, $f_{i,j} = |R_i\cap S_j|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness of CF-RS-Join/FVT w/ Length Filter based Early Stop]
\label{theorem}
Given two set collections $\mathcal{R}$ and $\mathcal{S}$, let \(path(s_i, s_j)\) denote a path starting at the node having set \(s_i\) to its parent, and the parent of its parent, until to the $n_\text{root}$ on the \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\). For any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\), and for any $a_k \in R_i$, %starting from $(a_k,|\text{seq}(a_k)|, L(a_k))\in \mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{S}}$, 
if \(n_{s_x}\) is the first node in \(path(L(a_k),n_\text{root}) = \langle L(a_k),L(a_k).par, \ldots, n_{s_x},\ldots, n_\text{root}\rangle\) having the following features: $|R_i|$ and $|S_x|$ do not meet the length filtering condition $\left \lceil t\times|R_i| \right \rceil \le |S_x| \le \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor $, then the value of $f_{i,x}$ remains untouched and the traversal early stops since the closer a node to the root, the larger the node's set size. After the traversal is completed, we have all set pairs with similarity no less than the threshold.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\sloppy
Based on Lemma \ref{lma:len filter}, traversing the tree \(\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{S}}\) without early stop can obtain exact R-S Join. For any \(R_i \in \mathcal{R}\), and for any $a_k \in R_i$, \(path(L(a_k),n_\text{root}))\) is the path to be traversed when no filter based early stop is concerned. If \(n_{s_x}\) is the first node in \(path(L(a_k),n_\text{root}))\) which does not satisfy $\left \lceil t\times|R_i| \right \rceil \le |S_x| \le \left \lfloor |R_i|/t \right \rfloor $, there must be $\text{Jaccard}(R_i,S_j)<t$ according to Lemma~\ref{lma:len filter}. Meanwhile, we have \(path(L(a_k), n_\text{root})) = path(L(a_k,n_{s_x})) + path(n_{s_x}, n_\text{root}) = \langle L(a_k), L(a_k).par, \ldots, n_{s_x},\ldots, n_\text{root})\rangle\), the tree in FVT has the following characteristics: the closer a node to the root, the larger the node's set size, then we have: no traversal along the path \(path(a_l,n_\text{root}) =\langle L(a_k),L(a_k).par, \ldots, n_\text{root})\rangle\) is needed to be conducted and the corresponding set pair similarity verification is removed. After the traversal is completed, we will have all set pairs with similarity no less than the threshold.
\end{proof}

Here is an example to illustrate CF-RS-Join/FVT obtains the exact R-S Join based on early stops using length filter. For the entry $\langle key = r_4, value = \{a_1, a_3\}\rangle$, and $t=0.6$, we have \(|R_4| = 2\). According to the length filtering strategy, the valid length interval of $s_j$ is $[\lceil2 \times 0.6\rceil,\lfloor2/0.6\rfloor] = [2,3]$. We scan the element table and find $n_{s_4}$ and \(n_{s_5}\) are indexed by \(L(a_1)\) and \(L(a_3)\), i.e., $\mathcal{N}=\{n_{s_4},n_{s_5}\}$. For the first element $n_{s_4}$, the set size \(|S_4| = 2\) is within the valid length interval, we have \(f_{4,4} = f_{4,4} + 1 = 1\). When the traversal goes to \(n_{s_2}\) in the FVT, it will stop since $|S_2| =5 > 3$. For this traversal, we have \(f_{4,4} = f_{4,5} = f_{4,3} =1\). After all traversal we have \(f_{4,4} = 1\), \(f_{4,5} = f_{4,3} =2\). From Figs. \ref{fig:sample R} and \ref{fig:sample S}, we can see that \(|R_4 \cap S_4| = |\{a_1\}| =1\), \(|R_4 \cap S_5| = |\{a_1,a_3\}| = 2\), and \(|R_4 \cap S_3| = |\{a_1,a_3\}| = 2\). 

Based on the aforementioned discussion, the benefits of CF-RS-Join/FVT, the R-S Join over the FVT, can be summarized as: (1) The element table facilitates fast element-set lookups; (2) The 2-stage filter-and-verification has been integrated into one single stage during the tree traversal, where the filter is used for an early stop, since nodes with bigger set sizes are closer to the root; (3) No more database scans for R-S Join once the FVT is constructed, and it remains in memory until computation finishes.

\textbf{Time complexity analysis.}
The computation workflow of CF-RS-Join/FVT described in Algorithm~\ref{alg2} includes the reorganization of one collection of sets, FVT construction, and R-S Join computation. Assuming that \(\mathcal{S}\) is selected for tree construction, the computation cost for data reorganization and the FVT construction is $O(|\mathcal{S}| \times \overline{|S|}  + l \times |\mathcal{S}'| \times \overline{|{\text{seq}({a_i})}|})$, where $\overline{|{S}|}$ is the mean set size of $S_j$, and $l$ is the average time of inserting a node into an FVT. The computation cost for traversing the tree and similarity calculation is $O(|\mathcal{R}|\times \overline{|{R}|}\times \overline{|{\text{seq}(a_i)}|} + |\mathcal{S}|)$, where $\overline{|{R}|}$ is the mean set size of $R_i$. 
In all, the time complexity of CF-RS-Join/FVT is 
$O( |\mathcal{S}| \times \overline{|{S}|} + l  \times |\mathcal{S}'| \times \overline{|{\text{seq}({a_i})}|} +  |\mathcal{R}|\times \overline{|{R}|}\times \overline{|{\text{seq}(a_i)}|} + |\mathcal{S}|)$.
Based on this, for the two sample set collections, \(\text{R}\) and \(\text{S}\), \(|\text{R}| \times \overline{|R|} = 4 \times 3 = 12\), \(|\text{S}| \times \overline{|S|}= 19\), \(\text{S}\) is chosen for the tree for lower computation cost.

