\section{Parallel and distributed CF-RS-Join with MapReduce}
\label{sec:FastAP-treeR-SJ}

When the scale of collections becomes large, since the constructed (L)FVT is kept in memory, the \(\mathcal{R}_f\) can be sliced into partitions, each of which will be dispatched to a core for exploiting multi-core architecture to accelerate the computation. When the data volume increases and the size of the (L)FVT exceeds the memory of one computer, cluster based distributed computing paradigms like MapReduce can be exploited to solve the problem. This section describes MapReduce based CF-RS-Join/FVT, i.e., \textit{MR-CF-RS-Join/FVT}, the same design philosophy can be applied to CF-RS-Join/LFVT.

The MR-CF-RS-Join/FVT includes 1 MapReduce job, \(\mathcal{R}_f\) and \(\mathcal{S}_f\) in Fig. \ref{fig: sample collections} are used to illustrate the computation process of the distributed R-S Join with FVTs, where the number of mappers and reducers are both configured to be 2 and $t=0.7$. The \texttt{map()} function slices $\mathcal{R}_f$ and $\mathcal{S}_f$ in \(\langle k,v \rangle\) format into \(l\) groups, each of which will be sent to a reducer for CF-RS-Join/FVT. A load-aware data partitioning strategy has been proposed to distribute $\text{R}_f$ and $\text{S}_f$ to $l$ reducers for load balancing, which follows the design philosophy in Length-BundleJoin~\cite{yang2020distributed}. Let $\psi (lb_l, rb_l, l)$ represent the %minimized 
estimated computational load of the most heavily loaded reducer, to distribute a subset of $\mathcal{S}_f$, i.e., $\{S_j \in \mathcal{S}_f \mid lb_l \le |S_j| \le rb_l\}$, and its corresponding subset of $\mathcal{R}_f$, i.e., $\lceil lb_l \times t\rceil \leq |R_i|\leq \lfloor rb_l /t\rfloor$, to the $l$ reducers, our target is to minimize the heaviest computational load of a node in the cluster for load balancing, i.e., min($\psi (lb_l, rb_l, l)$). To solve this problem, we use dynamic programming in \eqref{equ:dp}. 
\begin{equation}
\small
\label{equ:dp}
\psi (lb_l, rb_l, l) = 
\begin{cases}
    \displaystyle
    \min\limits_{ lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l-1} \max\left(
    \begin{aligned}
    &\psi (lb_l, i, l-1),\\
        &\text{load}(i+1, rb_l)
    \end{aligned}
    \right) & \text{if } l \geq 1 \\
    0 & 
    % \text{otherwise}
    \text{if } l= 0
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\text{load}(lb_l,rb_l)$ takes into account the computational load of the $S_j$ whose length is within $[lb_l,rb_l]$ and $R_i$ whose length is within $[ \lceil lb_l \times t\rceil , \lfloor rb_l /t\rfloor]$ on the same reducer. It is formulated as
\begin{eqnarray}
\small
\label{equ:load}
\text{load}(lb_l,rb_l) &=& \sum_{\left\lceil lb_l \times t \right\rceil \leq i \leq \left\lfloor {rb_l}/{t} \right \rfloor} i \times C_r(i) \times \sum_{ lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l } C_s(i)  \nonumber \\ 
& &  + \;\;\;
\sum_{lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l} i \times C_s(i)
\end{eqnarray}
%that computes sets with length within $[L,R]$. 
where $C_r(i)$ and  $C_s(i)$ denote the number of sets in $\mathcal{R}_f$ and $\mathcal{S}_f$ whose length is $i$. 
According to the time complexity, we denote the overhead of the building phase as $\sum_{lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l} i \times C_s(i)$, and denote the search phase as $\sum_{\left\lceil lb_l \times t \right\rceil \leq i \leq \left\lfloor {rb_l}/{t} \right\rfloor} i \times C_r(i) \times \sum_{lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l} C_s(i)$, where the $\overline{|{\text{seq}(a_i)}|} \leq \sum_{lb_l \leq i \leq rb_l} C_s(i)$.
Then, we use the $\psi(lb_l,rb_l,l)$ to calculate all solutions $\{\langle i,[lb_i,rb_i]\rangle \mid 1 \leq i \leq l\}$, which denotes the set length interval $[lb_i,rb_i]$ in $i$-th reducer.
\begin{sloppypar}
Fig. \ref{fig:FastAP-treeR-SJ} illustrates the computation process of the distributed R-S Join with CF-RS-Join/FVT over $\text{R}_f$ and $\text{S}_f$. We first read datasets, and compute the partitioning strategy $\{\langle i,[lb_i,rb_i]\rangle \mid 1 \leq i \leq l\}$ before the map task.
We obtain $l_{\min}=1$ and $l_{\max}=5$ to initialize $\psi(lb_i,rb_i,1)$ by $\text{load}(lb_i,rb_i)$ , then we calculate the $\psi(lb_l,rb_l,l)=\psi(lb_l,rb_l,2)$. 
%For example. when $l_j=2$, $\psi(l_{\min},l_j,2)=\max(\psi(1,2,1), \text{load}(3,5))=?$. 
Consequently, we have $\psi(lb_l,rb_l,2)=\max(\psi(1,3,1),\text{load}(4,5))=56$,  
the global partition, which is $\{\langle 2,[1,3]\rangle,\langle 1, [4,5] \rangle\}$, therefore, $s_1,s_2,r_1,r_3$ are routed to \textit{reducer2} while $s_3,s_4,s_5,s_6,r_2,r_3,r_4$ are routed to \textit{reducer1}.
\end{sloppypar}

In the map phase, the entries in \(\langle k,v \rangle\) format in \(\text{S}_f\) are partitioned into 2 groups respectively. For an entry \(\langle k = s_j,v = S_j \rangle \), let %the entry index be denoted as \(index(s_j)\) and 
the group id of it denote as \(gid(s_j)\), we obtain \(gid(s_j)\) of each entry from the solutions $\{\langle i,[lb_i,rb_i]\rangle \mid 1 \leq i \leq N\}$, i.e., if $lb_i\leq |S_j| \leq rb_i$, its \(gid(s_j)\) is $i$.
The output of the \texttt{map()} function is in the format \(\langle gid(s_j), v = \langle'S',s_j, S_j\rangle\rangle\), where the char \('S'\) is the tag used to characterize the corresponding entry is from \(\text{S}_f\) or \(\text{R}_f\). 
For each entry in \(\text{R}_f\), the \texttt{map()} function will output 1 or 2 intermediate results according to the entry length. 
For example, the entry with \(r_3\) in \(\text{R}_f\), the \textit{mapper1} outputs 2 intermediate results: \(\langle0,\langle'R',r_3,R_3\rangle\rangle\) and \(\langle1,\langle'R',r_3,R_3\rangle\rangle\), since the $r_i$ length interval in \textit{reducer1} is $[1,4]$ and the \textit{reducer2} is $[3,7]$.  

Then \texttt{reduce()} functions execute on \(l\) reducers independently, each will get a group of data with the same key
% id of \(\mathcal{R}_f\) and \(\mathcal{S}_f\) 这里应该不对
from the mappers. Then each reducer performs CF-RS-Join/FVT, where 15 nodes and 16 nodes of FVTs are accessed in \textit{reducer1} and \textit{reducer2} respectively, with almost balanced load.

\section{Performance Evaluation} \label{sec:Experimental Evaluation}

CF-RS-Join algorithms improve runtime efficiency by compressing one collection of sets in memory for R-S Join computation, integrating the filter and verification into one stage, so as to remove the candidate pair generation, reduce I/O operations, and thus improve overall performance. However, these algorithms introduce runtime overheads for dynamic tree construction, keeping the entire tree in memory leads to high memory demands and limit the concurrency. In this section, we evaluate MR-CF-RS-Join algorithms performance using three metrics (runtime, memory usage, and disk consumption) through 2 comparative experiments: MR-CF-RS-Joins vs. CF-RS-Joins, and MR-CF-RS-Joins vs. existing selective distributed algorithms.
