\section{Introduction}

% Outline: 
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Network theory, large networks, graphon theory
% \item What are graphons and why they are relevant
% \item Why graphon estimation is important
% \item Why low-rank graphons are important.
% \item Why estimating low-rank graphons in the operator norm is a new problem. Connect to existing literature
% \item Contributions
% \item Organization
% \end{itemize}

Large networks are ubiquitous in today's world, present in various systems such as social networks, epidemic models, power grids, the Internet of Things, and more. These networked systems possess a unique characteristic: the agents within them are interconnected via the network. Consequently, the efficiency of algorithms operating on these systems scales with the size of the network, denoted as $n$.
For instance, consider linear-quadratic network games, where computing a Nash equilibrium of the game exhibits a worst-case complexity of $O(n^3)$ \cite{galeotti_interventions}. In practical scenarios,  $n$  can reach substantial magnitudes; for instance, Facebook boasts over 2 billion users. Consequently, for computationally intensive tasks, such algorithms can become practically intractable.
To address this challenge, the theory of graphons has emerged as a valuable tool. For example, in the context of network games, graphons have been employed to mitigate computational complexities \cite{Parise_Ozdaglar_Econometrica}. 


Formally, a graphon is a measurable function $W$ given by $W : [0,1]^2 \rightarrow [0,1]$. A graphon can either be interpreted as a limit of a sequence of finite networks, or as a probabilistic model that generates random finite networks \cite{lovasz}. While, traditionally, networked systems   have been modeled on finite networks, these models have been extended to graphons, e.g., \cite{Parise_Ozdaglar_Econometrica} extended network games to graphon games. These works have shown that modeling networked systems as graphon systems has three benefits: (i) graphons provide a mathematically elegant, unified framework for representing and comparing networked systems of various sizes, (ii) a graphon system can closely approximate the behaviour of a networked system if the network has been sampled from the graphon, (iii) under certain conditions on the graphon, the dimension of some computational problems involving the networked system can be significantly reduced, thus leading to computationally efficient methods. 


Crucially, the works on graphon games mentioned above assumed that  the true graphon that generates finite networks is known to the planner. 
However, in practical scenarios, the true graphon remains unknown. Therefore, the task of estimating a graphon from a sampled network emerges as a significant problem extensively explored in the literature \cite{chatterjee2012,gao2014rate,klopp2017,klopp2019,xu2017}. In connection to our motivating applications, in this study, our focus lies on providing a \textit{low-rank estimator} for the underlying unknown a  graphon based on a sampled network.
There are two reasons for this: (i) It has been observed that for the adjacency matrix of networks that are observed in practice, usually, most of the eigenvalues are close to zero. Thus, these networks can be well-approximated by graphons that have a low rank \cite{caines}. (ii) There is evidence in the literature that low-rank graphons can significantly reduce the computational complexity of problems in networked systems. For example \cite{Parise_Ozdaglar_Econometrica} 
showed that if the graphon has rank $r$, then the problem of computing an optimal intervention in a linear-quadratic graphon game can be reduced to a semidefinite program (SDP) of $2$ variables and a linear matrix inequality (LMI) constraint involving a matrix of dimension $r+1$. This evidence motivates us to look for an estimator
that has two desirable properties: (P1) it is close to the underlying graphon that generated the network in operator norm, 
%observed/sampled network  
(P2) it has low rank. 

%Our contributions can be divided into two parts: (i) estimation of the underlying graphon from an observed network, and (ii) application of this estimator to graphon games.
The problem of estimating a graphon from a network has been studied in the existing literature mostly using the Frobenius norm and the cut norm as measures of estimation error, see, e.g., \cite{gao2014rate,klopp2017,klopp2019}. Unlike these previous works we investigate the problem of estimating a graphon in the \emph{operator norm} looking for a low rank estimator. To this end, we use the singular value thresholding (SVT) estimator, see, e.g., \cite{chatterjee2012}. Given a network of size \( n \) sampled from an unknown graphon, our initial step involves constructing a low-rank approximation of the adjacency matrix. We achieve this by selecting only those terms from the singular value decomposition of the adjacency matrix that correspond to singular values equal to or greater than a threshold value. Subsequently, we derive our estimated graphon from the resulting matrix as a piecewise constant graphon. 
Under assumptions that the unknown graphon possesses certain structural characteristics, such as being a step function or a smooth function, we establish bounds on the operator norm distance between the true unknown graphon and the estimated graphon. Furthermore, we provide bounds on the rank of the estimated graphon and demonstrate that it exhibits slow growth with increasing \( n \).
 We illustrate the interest of such results with  application to   the social welfare problem in graphon games.
 %and  graphon control of large-scale networks of linear systems. 

%In particular, we derive bounds on the suboptimality of intervention in the social welfare problem in graphon games when applying a solution derived from the estimated graphon. The bounds are derived in terms of the operator norm of the difference between the true unknown graphon and the estimated graphon. Pervious results, e.g., \cite{Parise_Ozdaglar_Econometrica} derive a bound on the suboptimality of the social welfare intervention designed from the true graphon when applied to a network sampled from the graphon. Using low-rank estimated graphon instead of the true graphon is, in the first place, feasible as the underlying graphon is  unknown and, in the second place, is computational advantageous. We illustrate our results using numerical examples. 


The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section \ref{section: preliminaries}, we introduce some preliminaries and our key estimation problem. In Section \ref{section: motivating applications}, we provide examples of  applications to graphon games. 
% and derive bounds on the suboptimality of the solutions obtained from a perturbed graphon, \blue{quantified via the operator norm of the difference between the two graphons}.
 In Section \ref{section: estimation}, we introduce our estimator, and derive bounds on (i) the operator norm of the difference between the estimated graphon and the true graphon (ii) the rank of the estimated graphon. In Section \ref{section:simulations}, we apply our results on graphon estimation to the problem of targeted interventions in networks games and provide numerical experiments to illustrate our foundings. The proofs of all the results are given in the appendix. 




