\subsection{ Optimal Control of Network Dynamical Systems }


	The optimal control problem on a dynamical system involves choosing a control input that minimizes a total running cost and a terminal cost of the system, defined as a function of the system state and the control input. For large scale networked systems, this problem is intractable, even if the system is linear and the cost function is quadratic. To address this issue, the theory of graphon dynamical systems was developed in \cite{graphon_LQR_gao, low_rank_LQR_gao}. Graphon dynamical systems are limits of networked dynamical systems as the number of nodes in the network tends to infinity. Next, we briefly review graphon dynamical systems and the associated optimal control problem.

	\subsection*{3.2.1 Graphon dynamical systems and optimal control}
	In a graphon dynamical system, the set $[0,1]$ represents the set of all subsystems. Let $x_{t}(v) \in \mathbb{R}$ be the state of subsystem $v \in[0,1]$ at time $t \in[0, T]$. Then, $x_{t} \in L_{2}[0,1]$ is the state of the graphon dynamical system. Similarly, let $u_{t}(v) \in \mathbb{R}$ be the control input of subsystem $v$. In this paper we focus on linear graphon dynamical systems with dynamics	
	\begin{equation}\label{def:lgds}
		\dot{x}_{t}=(\alpha \mathbb{I}+\mathbb{A}) x_{t}+(\beta \mathbb{I}+\mathbb{B}) u_{t}, \quad t \in[0, \infty) 
	\end{equation}
		where $\mathbb{I}$ is the identity operator on $L_{2}[0,1]$ and $\mathbb{A}$ and $\mathbb{B}$ are the operators associated with graphon $A$ (coupling the states) and graphon $B$ (coupling the inputs).

	Next, suppose that we want to minimize a cost associated with the state and the input of the graphon dynamical system. We consider cost functions that are quadratic in the state and the control input. Specifically, we are interested in solving the problem


	\begin{align}\label{def:LQR}
		\min _{u: u_{t} \in L_{2}[0,1] \forall t \in[0, \infty)} & J_{(A, B)}(u):=\int_{0}^{\infty}\left(\left\langle x_{t}, \mathbb{Q} x_{t}\right\rangle+ \Vert u_t \Vert^2\right) d t  \\
		\text { s.t. } & \dot{x}_{t}=(\alpha \mathbb{I}+\mathbb{A}) x_{t}+(\beta \mathbb{I}+\mathbb{B}) u_{t}, \quad t \in[0, \infty)\nonumber
	\end{align}
		where $\mathbb{Q}$ is Hermitian, positive-definite operators on $L_{2}[0,1]$. Problem \eqref{def:LQR} is referred to as the linear-quadratic regulator (LQR) problem.



%	\subsection*{3.2.2 Challenges}
	Solving problem \eqref{def:LQR} presents two challenges similar to the ones in graphon games: i) the graphons $A$ and $B$ are unknown, and ii) problem \eqref{def:LQR} is, in general, computationally intractable. Similar to the solution proposed for graphon games, we assume that we can use two networks sampled from the graphons $A$ and $B$, respectively, to estimate the unknown graphons and solve problem \eqref{def:LQR} for the estimated graphons. We can then implement the derived control input on the original graphon dynamical system. Theorem 7 in \cite{graphon_LQR_gao} gives a bound on the suboptimality of the cost of such a solution, in terms of the operator norm difference of the original and sampled graphons. This result can be extended to obtained a bound  on the suboptimality, in terms of the operator norm difference of the original graphon and its low-rank approximation provided by \eqref{eq: HTE definition}.

	To address the problem ii), we use a result from \cite{graphon_LQR_gao} which shows that, if the graphon $A$ has a low-rank, and the graphons $B, Q$ and $S$ are polynomial functions of $A$, then, the problem \eqref{def:LQR} can be solved by solving $r+1$ scalar Riccati equations. Thus, the complexity of the problem depends on the rank of the graphon $A$. So to obtain computationally efficient method to solve problem \eqref{def:LQR} for general graphons
	  we need a low rank graphon estimator satisfying properties (P1) and (P2).


 Application of the framework described above to the optimal network control problem requires some sensitivity analysis of the problem~\eqref{def:LQR}. The solution of this problem can be obtained in three steps~\cite{Infinite_dim_control_bensoussan, low_rank_LQR_gao}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find the solution of the following Riccati euqation:
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq: riccati equation}
        \begin{cases}
            \dot{\bbP}(t) & = \bbA^\top \bbP(t) + \bbP(t) \bbA - \bbP \bbB \bbB^\top \bbP + \bbQ; \\
            \bbP(0) & = 0;
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    Denote the solution by $\bbP^*$.
    \item Find the solution of the differential equation:
    \begin{align}
    \label{eq: equation xt}
        \dot{x}_t & = [\bbA - \bbB \bbB^\top \bbP^*(\infty)] x_t,
    \end{align}
    for some given $x_0$.
    \item Find the optimal control $u^*_t$:
    \begin{align*}
        u^*_t = - \bbB^\top \bbP^*(\infty) x_t.
    \end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
The optimal value of $J(u)$ can be expressed as the scalar product $\langle \bbP^*(\infty) x_0, x_0 \rangle$.

\begin{remark}
    In the case of finite horizon $T$ in the optimal control problem, the operator $\bbP^*(\infty)$ in the above equations should be replaced by a time-dependent operator $\bbP^*(T - t)$. 
\end{remark}
Note that the solution $u^*_t$ depends on the operator $\bbP^*(\infty)$ rather than the whole solution $\bbP(t)$ of the equation~\eqref{eq: riccati equation}. The operator $\bbP^*(\infty)$ can be found by solving the following algebraic Riccati equation:
\begin{align}
\label{eq: algebraic Riccati equation}
    0 = \bbA^\top \bbP^*(\infty) + \bbP^*(\infty) \bbA - \bbP^*(\infty) \bbB \bbB^\top \bbP^*(\infty) + \bbQ.
\end{align}


If one uses a control $\widehat{u}_t$ instead of $u^*_t$, where $\widehat{u}_t$ is the optimal solution to problem~\eqref{def:LQR} with $\bbA, \bbB, \bbQ$ replaced by $\widehat{\bbA}, \widehat{\bbB}, \widehat{\bbQ}$, then one should perform a sensitivity analysis of the algebraic Riccati equation~\eqref{eq: algebraic Riccati equation} and the differential equation~\eqref{eq: equation xt}. The sensitivity analysis of the Riccati equation in the matrix setting was performed by Kenney and Caines~\cite{sensitivity_riccati_solution}
%We believe that their analysis can be adapted to the case of Hilbert--Schimdt operators, but it would yield only 
with asymptotic guarantees on the difference
\begin{align*}
    J_{(A, B)}(\widehat{u}) - J_{(A, B)}(u^*)
\end{align*}
as $\widehat{\bbA}, \widehat{\bbB}, \widehat{\bbQ}$ tend to $\bbA, \bbB, \bbQ$.
%Establishing non-asymptotic guarantees for this problem is a more challenging task and lies out of the scope of this paper.

