\section{Introduction}
With significant advancements in diffusion models\cite{sohl2015deep, ho2020denoising, dhariwal2021diffusion}, the deep learning community has witnessed remarkable progress in high-fidelity content generation \cite{ couairon2022diffedit, hertz2022prompt, wu2024diffusion}, while the distinguishability between synthetic and authentic content has become increasingly challenging. 
Specifically, the latent diffusion model (LDM) \cite{rombach2022high} has demonstrated unparalleled efficacy in controllable image manipulation. 
Distinguished by its compressed latent space operation, LDM achieves efficient computation through iterative denoising processes employing a U-net architecture.
Furthermore, users could input user-defined manipulation region masks into diffusion inpainting models to achieve fine-grained control of manipulation detail, which improves the authenticity of manipulation results.

\begin{figure}
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ introduce.pdf}
	\caption{The proactive defense against the misuse of diffusion models guided by unknown conditions.}
	\label{fig1}
\end{figure}
However, these advancements raise growing ethical concerns about the malicious use of diffusion-based image manipulation techniques.
Image manipulation models\cite{chefer2023attend, wang2023imagen, yenphraphai2024image, zhang2023adding}, capable of producing extraordinarily lifelike and persuasive outputs, can be misused maliciously, including producing fabricated news, disseminating false information, and crafting misleading imagery, as shown in Figure \ref{fig1} (top row).
For instance, open-source diffusion models\cite{ho2020denoising, brooks2023instructpix2pix, sohl2015deep, bansal2023universal} enable effortless photo manipulation to fabricate scenarios like falsely portraying a celebrity's arrest. As these models become increasingly sophisticated, mitigating such risks and establishing protective solutions against malicious manipulation becomes critical\cite{phan2025latent}.

To mitigate the potential risks associated with the misuse of diffusion models,
proactive defense methods\cite{salman2023raising, liang2023adversarial, liang2023mist, yu2024step, son2024disrupting, liu2024disrupting, jeong2024faceshield, ozden2024optimization, lo2024distraction, zheng2023understanding} based on adversarial perturbation have shown promise recently. However, most mainstream methods do not consider proactive defense under unknown tampering conditions, as shown in Figure \ref{fig1} (middle row), and how malicious users modify the protected images is unknown in the real-world scenario. The main factors of this process contain manipulation region masks and initial latent states used in the diffusion process. 
Diffusionguard\cite{choi2024diffusionguard} improves the protection of adversarial perturbations against manipulation guided by invisible masks by introducing mask augmentation into optimization. 
However, the protection of existing methods against tampering with resampling initial latent states is still limited, which gives malicious users the opportunity of high-quality unauthorized image manipulation by trying different initial hidden states. 
Therefore, we motivate our work by protecting images against malicious tampering under unknown conditions.

To address these challenges, this paper presents Anti-Inpainting, a proactive defense method to protect images from the threats posed by diffusion-based inpainters under unknown conditions, as shown in Figure \ref{fig1} (bottom row). 
The diffusion model generates predicted noise through the U-net module. U-net will pay attention to different levels of features for different inputs, and the more concerning features will have a more significant gradient corresponding to the predicted noise in U-net. 
To improve the disruption effect of the adversarial perturbation on fake content generation, we extract these multi-level deep features to replace the predicted noise as the attack target of our method.
Subsequently, we introduce multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation into the adversarial optimization process, improving adversarial perturbations' protective capability against manipulation guided by invisible masks.
Additionally, the latent state is also one of the inputs of the U-net module, which is random and significantly impacts the prediction noise of the U-net module. 
Therefore, we design a selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy to reduce the impact of the randomness of latent states on the protective performance of adversarial samples by targeted optimization on the latent states that cause protective failure.
We summarize our main contributions as follows:
\begin{itemize}
	\item We proposed Anti-Inpainting, a proactive defense method, to generate adversarial perturbations that protect images from diffusion-based inpainting models under unknown conditions.
	\item We present a multi-level deep feature extractor to obtain intricate features. 
	Additionally, we propose a multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation to improve the transferability of adversarial perturbations across unknown conditions. 
	We design a selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy to achieve effective protection against malicious manipulation under diverse latent states and efficient adversarial optimization.
	\item Extensive experiments demonstrate that Anti-Inpainting can protect images effectively from diffusion-based inpainting models under unknown conditions and is robust under diverse image purification. 
\end{itemize}
\section{Related Work}
%\subsection{Diffusion-based Manipulation Models}
%Diffusion-based manipulation models can be divided into two categories: text-to-image generation models based on fine-tuning \cite{gal2022image, hu2022lora, ruiz2023dreambooth} and image editing models based on inference\cite{rombach2022high, zhang2023adding}. Text-to-image generation models based on fine-tuning are not as user-friendly as the inference-based image editing model due to their requirements on computing devices and the technical threshold for users. The inference-based image editing model includes only text-guided image editing and image inpainting, which are additionally guided by manipulation region masks. Image inpainting models can achieve meticulous control of the tampering details because they take the manipulation region mask as input, so malicious users can more easily use it to generate disinformation.
%Diffusion-based manipulation models include text-guided image editing models and image inpainting models guided by manipulation region masks. Image inpainting models can achieve meticulous control of the tampering details because they take the manipulation region mask as input, so malicious users more easily use it to generate disinformation.
%\subsection{Proactive Defense Methods}
Recently, researchers\cite{liang2023mist, wang2024simac, xue2023toward, xu2024perturbing, van2023anti, ahn2024imperceptible, mi2024visual} have proposed several adversarial perturbations against diffusion-based inpainting models to protect images from unauthorized manipulation. PhotoGuard\cite{salman2023raising}, as an adversarial defense framework, employs dual perturbation strategies (encoder attack and diffusion attack) to disrupt model perception through latent space manipulation. 
While effective against known guidance conditions (e.g., specific manipulation region mask), its protection exhibits limited conditional transferability to unseen manipulation scenarios, particularly when attackers employ post-processing techniques like manual mask generation.
\begin{figure*}
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ model_struct1.pdf}
	\caption{Overview of Anti-Inapinting. Our approach includes three components: 1) multi-level deep feature extractor; 2) multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation; 3) selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy.}
	\label{fig2}
\end{figure*}
To address malicious manipulation guided by unknown conditions, Choi et al.\cite{choi2024diffusionguard} proposed DiffusionGuard with contour shrinking mask augmentation. 
However, the substantial geometric modifications caused by mask augmentation affect the semantic information of the masks, thereby weakening the protective performance of adversarial perturbation.

Parallel research by Yu et al. uncovers critical vulnerabilities in reverse processes of diffusion models through their proposed mean fluctuation attack (MFA) \cite{yu2024step}. 
This attack strategically targets the temporal dynamics of denoising steps by identifying the most vulnerable time step in the diffusion scheduler.
However, their methodology overlooks a crucial component in U-Net's input architecture: the stochastic initial latent states sampled from  Gaussian distribution ~$N(0, I)$~. 
Our experimental validation reveals a significant correlation between latent selection and adversarial protective efficacy. Specifically,  protective efficiency is improved by selecting latent states with threshold selection on InpaintBench and CelebA-HQ.
\section{Method}
We introduce Anti-Inpainting, a proactive defense method, which safeguards images from manipulation by the inpainting model through a multi-level deep feature extractor, multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation, and selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy, of which the algorithm flow is shown in Figure \ref{fig2}. This section introduces the basic adversarial attack framework used in our method (Section~\ref{Preliminaries}). Next, we explain how we extract multi-level deep features during the denoising process (Section ~\ref{Multi-Level Deep Feature Extractor}). Subsequently, we introduce multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation designed to enhance the protective performance against manipulation under unknown conditions (Section \ref{Multi-Scale Semantic-Preserving Data Augmentation}). We present a selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy (Section \ref{Selection-Based Distribution Deviation Optimization Strategy}).
\subsection{Preliminaries}\label{Preliminaries}
\paragraph{Editing models.} Diffusion editing models \cite{brooks2023instructpix2pix} are diffusion models specially designed to generate images conditioned on clean images ~$ I $~ and text prompts ~$ \mathscr{T}$~. These models incorporate clean image embedding extracted from an image encoder ~$ \mathcal{E}(\cdot) $~ like VAE\cite{kingma2013auto} to guide the image generation process. Given a pair of an image ~$ I $~ and text prompts~$ \mathscr{T} $ ~, these models employ a noise prediction model~$\epsilon_\theta(\cdot)$~ \cite{ronneberger2015u} and achieve image editing by multiple denoise processes, which can be expressed as Equation \ref{eq1}:
\begin{equation}
	z_{t-1} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\left( z_t - \frac{1-\alpha_t}{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}} \epsilon_\theta(z_t, \mathcal{E} (I), t, clip(\mathscr{T}) )\right) + \sigma_t {n}
	\label{eq1}
\end{equation}
where ~$t$~is the timestep of denoising process, and ~$ z_t $~ is the latent state after ~$ T-t $~(~$T$~ is the number of sampling steps) steps of denoising process. ~$ clip(\cdot)$~ is the text encoder\cite{radford2021learning} in the diffusion model. ~$ \alpha_t$~, ~$ \bar\alpha_t$~ and ~$ \sigma_t $~ are the hyperparametric of diffusion models. ~$ {n} $~ is sampled from normal distribution~$ N(0,1) $~. 
\paragraph{Inpainting models.} In addition to text-guided editing models, inpainting models that modify specific regions of a given image are also significant. 
Specifically, the manipulation region of the given image ~$ I $~ is masked, and the image encoder ~$ \mathcal{E}(\cdot) $~ generates image embeddings from the masked image~$ I_{masked} $~. 
During the inpainting process, the image embedding and the mask are concated to the latent state ~$ z_t $~ as an input of noise predictor ~$\epsilon_\theta(\cdot)$~.
The inpainting process by multiple denoise processes is expressed as follows:
\begin{equation}
	z_{t-1} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha_t}}\left( z_t - \frac{1-\alpha_t}{\sqrt{1-\bar\alpha_t}} \epsilon_\theta(z_t, \mathcal{E}(I_{masked}), M, t, clip(\mathscr{T})) \right) + \sigma_t {n}
	\label{eq3}
\end{equation}
\paragraph{The proactive defense framework against diffusion models.} Adversarial perturbations are designed to prevent the diffusion model from unauthorized manipulation. Given a clean image  ~$ I $~ and a diffusion model, the adversarial perturbation ~$ \delta $~ is added into the clean image ~$ I $~ so that the protected image deceives the model. These perturbations ~$ \delta $~ are typically limited to be imperceptible to human eyes through constrained optimization using norm bound~$ \eta $~. Some researchers consider crafting an adversarial perturbation to enforce the inpainting models to generate low-quality images. 
These works either maximize the diffusion model training loss or perturb the predicted noise of the single-step denoising process as follows:
\begin{equation} 
	\delta  = \mathop{\arg\max}\limits_{||\delta||_{\infty}\leq \eta}~ 
	\mathbb{E}_{ \epsilon \sim {N}(0, I) } \left\| \epsilon - \epsilon_{\theta}(z,  \mathcal{E}(I_{masked} + \delta), M,t, clip(\mathscr{T})) \right\|_2^2 
	\label{eq4}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	\delta = \mathop{\arg\min}\limits_{||\delta||_{\infty}\leq \eta}~ 
	\left\| \epsilon_{\theta}(z, \mathcal{E}(I_{masked} + \delta), M, t, clip(\mathscr{T})) \right\|_1
	\label{eq5}
\end{equation}
\subsection{Multi-Level Deep Feature Extractor}
\label{Multi-Level Deep Feature Extractor}
The optimization objective of current mainstream methods is to affect the distribution of the predicted noise of the denoising process in diffusion models. Predicted noise only represents the high-level feature of the U-net module but not the low-level feature. However, both high-level and low-level features are worthy of attention, and the ablation experiment (Subsection \ref{feature selection}) also proves that simultaneously affecting the distribution of features at different levels is conducive to improving the protective effect of adversarial perturbations. Therefore, we propose a multi-level deep feature extractor to obtain multi-level features by extracting the block-wise features of the U-net module in diffusion models, as shown in Figure \ref{fig2}.

Firstly, we generate the input tensor and guidance condition ~$ c $~ for the first block~$\epsilon^{0}$~ of the U-net module. The input tensor includes the embeding of the input image ~$ \mathcal{E}(I_{input}) $~ , the interpreted input mask ~$ inter(M_{input})  $~ and the latent state ~$ z $~ ; guidance condition ~$ c $~ includes timestep of the denoising process~$ t $~ and the embeding of text prompt ~$ {clip} (\mathscr{T})$~. 
\begin{equation}
{c} =  {concat}(t,  {clip} (\mathscr{T}))
\label{eq6}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
%	{Input} =  {concat} ( inter(M ),\mathcal{E} (x \odot M ),z_{t})\\
	f_{0} = \epsilon^{0}(z, \mathcal{E}(I_{input}), inter(M_{input}), c)
\label{eq7}
\end{equation}
where the latent state ~$ z $~ is sampled from normal distribution~$ N(0,1) $~ , and timestep ~$t$~ is sampled from uniform distribution ~$ U(710,730)$~ . As demonstrated by equation \ref{eq8}, we obtain the intermediate variable from the U-net module.
\begin{equation}
f_{i} = 
\left
	\{\begin{array}{lll}
		\epsilon_{down}^{i}\left(f_{i-1}, {c}\right), \quad   & if & 6>i>0 \\
		\epsilon_{mid}^{i}\left(f_{i-1}, {c}\right),\quad  ~& if & i=6 \\
		\epsilon_{up}^{i}(f_{i-1}, f_{10-i}, {c}),\quad ~& if & 11>i>6
	\end{array}
\right.
\label{eq8}
\end{equation}
where i is the number corresponding to the block ~$ \epsilon $~, ~$ \epsilon_{down}^{i}$~ is the downsampling block of U-net, ~$ \epsilon_{mid}^{i}$~ is the middle layer of U-net, and ~$ \epsilon_{up}^{i}$~ is the upsampling block of U-net. 
And then ~$ f_{11}$~ is the output of post-processing module ~$ \epsilon^{11}$~ in U-net. Next, we combine the intermediate variable of each U-net block and define the whole process as multi-level deep feature extraction~$  \phi $~, which is captured by Equation \ref{eq9}.
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
	F &= \phi \left(z, \mathcal{E}(I_{input}), M_{input}, t, clip(\mathscr{T})\right)  \\
	&= concat\left(f_{0},f_{1}, f_{2}, f_{3}, \ldots \ldots f_{11}\right)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq9}
\end{equation}
As the latent state ~$ z $~ is sampled from normal distribution~$ N(0,1) $~, we compute multi-level deep features mean ~$ \overline{F_{{ori}}} $~based on multiple latent states ~$ z $~  as the feature representation of the clean image ~$ I $~, and compute the distribution radius~$R_{ori}$~ to quantify the dispersion of feature on the clean image~$ I $~.
\begin{equation}
	\left.\overline{F_{{ori}}}=E_{z \in N(0,1)} [ \phi \left(z, \mathcal{E}(I), M , t, \mathscr{T}\right)\right]
\label{eq10}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
	\left.R_{ori}=E_{z \in N(0,1)} [ \| \phi \left(z, \mathcal{E}(I),  M, t, \mathscr{T} \right)-\overline{F_{{ori}}} \|_{2}\right]
\label{eq11}
\end{equation}
\subsection{Multi-Scale Semantic-Preserving Data Augmentation}
\label{Multi-Scale Semantic-Preserving Data Augmentation}
Current mainstream methods can only protect images from manipulation guided by known conditions, but guidance conditions leveraged by malicious users to manipulate images are unpredictable and multifaceted. 
Therefore, protection from manipulation based on unknown conditions is vital. 
Inspired by DiffusionGuard\cite{choi2024diffusionguard}, we found that using an augmented mask as one of the guidance conditions in the process of perturbation generation can significantly improve the protective performance against image manipulation guided by unknown conditions. However, the protective performance of DiffusionGuard is still limited due to the significant impact of contour-shrinking data augmentation on the mask, destroying the semantic information of the mask. Therefore, to protect the background of the object targeted by the mask from malicious manipulation, we propose multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation that preserves the semantic information of the mask while enhancing the diversity of the mask used in the adversarial optimization, as shown in equation \ref{eq12}. 
\begin{equation}
M_{aug}=
\left\{
	\begin{array}{lll}
		  \Omega \ ( \ M , n) \ , \quad   &if& n \geq 0 \\

		  \zeta \ ( \ \Omega \ ( \ \zeta \ ( \ M \ ) \ , -n) \ ) \ ,~&if& n<0
	\end{array}
\right.
\label{eq12}
\end{equation}
where ~$ {\Omega} (\cdot) $~ \footnotemark is mask dilation operation, and ~${\zeta} (\cdot)$~ is mask invertion operation. And then ~$ n $~ is sampled from ~$- \gamma $~ to ~$\gamma $~as the kernel size of data augmentation (~$ \gamma $~ is the hyperparameter augmentation intensity), and ~$ M $~ is the known mask. 
When ~$ n  \geq 0 $~, we apply a dilation operation with a kernel size of ~$ n \times n $~ on the content area to increase its area. 
When ~$n \textless 0$~, we apply a dilation operation with a kernel size of  ~$ n \times n $~  on the background area to reduce the mask area. 
It is important to note that the shape of the augmented mask remains unaffected mainly, thereby ensuring that the semantic object targeted by the mask remains unchanged when we perform dilation operations on either the retained or modified areas.
\footnotetext{This function is achieved by cv2.dilate$( \cdot  )$ from: \url{https://docs.opencv.org/}}
\subsection{Selection-based Distribution Deviation Optimization Strategy}
\label{Selection-Based Distribution Deviation Optimization Strategy}
%The current mainstream methods involve designing a loss function to make the diffusion models' adversarial predicted noise deviate from the original predicted noise distribution. For example, MFA  minimizes the mean of the predicted noise to deviate the adversarial predicted noise from the original distribution. 
%However, these methods have the problem of limited deviation efficiency. In the case of limited perturbation budget~$ \eta $~, the adversarial perturbation ~$  \delta $~ generated by these methods will fail to protect images because the deviation amplitude of the predicted noise is inadequate. 
%In addition, 
Since initial latent states significantly impact the output of diffusion models, the protective capability of adversarial perturbation under multiple latent states, which is related to random seeds, merits consideration. However, current proactive defense frameworks, constrained by overfitting on singular latent states, demonstrate restricted protective efficacy under scenarios involving multiple initial latent states.
To address these limitations, we propose a selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy.

Firstly, we reduce the unnecessary perturbation budget consumption by skipping optimization based on adversarial feature ~$F_{j}^{i}$~ deviated from the original distribution and improve the protective effect against the perturbation in the case of fixed perturbation budget~$\eta$~. As shown in Figure \ref{fig2}, we use the multi-level deep feature extractor ~$ \phi $~ to obtain the feature distribution of the clean image under different latent states ~$ z $~ as the original distribution. At the same time, we compute the mean~$ \overline{F_{{ori}}} $~ and radius ~$R_{ori} $~of the original distribution.
We then leverage a multi-level deep feature extractor ~$ \phi $~ to obtain the features of the protected image~$ I_{adv}^{i} $~ corresponding to the data augmented mask ~$  M_{aug} $~ after ~$i $~iterations of update, as follows:
\begin{equation}
	F_{j}^{i}=\phi \left(z_j, \mathcal{E}(I_{adv}^{i}), M_{aug} , t, \mathscr{T}\right) 
	\label{eq13}
\end{equation}
where ~$z_j$~ is the initial latent sampled by ~$j$~ times.
We calculate the distance  ~$ D_{adv} $~between the adversarial features ~$ F_{j}^{i} $~ and the original feature mean~$ \overline{F_{ori}} $~ , as follows:
\begin{equation}
	D_{adv}=\left\|F_{j}^{i}-\overline{F_{ori}}\right\|_{2} 
	\label{eq14}
\end{equation}
By comparing the magnitude between the adversarial feature distance ~$ D_{adv} $~and the ~$ \tau $~ times of original distribution radius~$ R_{ori}$~(~$ \tau $~ is the hyperparameter threshold), we judge whether the adversarial feature~$ F_{j}^{i} $~ is close to the original feature distribution.
As shown in Figure \ref{fig2}, in the mask-feature-latent space, the adversarial feature ~$ F_{j}^{i} $~ corresponding to the latent state ~$ z_j $~ has deviated from the original feature distribution, so we resample the latent state ~$ z_{j+1} $~ to avoid the inefficient optimization. 
%If the resampled adversarial feature~$ F_{j+1}^{i} $~ does not deviate from the original distribution, we generate the protected image ~$ I_{adv}^{i+1} $~, and the adversarial feature~$ F_{j+1}^{i+1} $~ of the protected image ~$ I_{adv}^{i+1}$~ deviates from the original distribution. 
If the resampled adversarial feature~$ F_{j+1}^{i} $~ does not deviate from the original distribution, we deviate the adversarial feature~$ F_{j+1}^{i} $~ from the original distribution and update the protected image ~$ I_{adv}^{i} $~ in the iteration ~$i$~ of adversarial optimization.
In addition, to find an efficient optimization direction, we will maximize the distance between the feature of the protected image~$F_{j}^{i}$~ and the clean feature mean~$ \overline{F_{ori}}  $~. The loss function is shown as follows:
\begin{equation}
	L_{adv} =-\left\|\left(F_{j}^{i}-\overline{F_{ori}}\right) \right\|_{2}^{2}
	\label{eq15}
\end{equation}

We minimize the loss function in each iteration via projected gradient descent(PGD)\cite{madry2017towards} to update the protected image.
