\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\paragraph{Datasets.} We conducted quantitative tests of our method and the baseline on InpaintGuardBench \cite{choi2024diffusionguard} and CelebA-HQ \cite{karras2017progressive}. InpaintGuardBench \cite{choi2024diffusionguard} consists of 42 images, each containing one visible mask, four invisible masks, and 10 text prompts. We also selected the first 100 images from CelebA-HQ \cite{karras2017progressive} for testing, using the corresponding skin mask in CelebAMask-HQ \cite{lee2020maskgan} as the visible masks and manually generating four invisible masks for each image. The manual methods for generating masks include drawing with a circular brush on the original image and using simple shape overlays on the protected image. Finally, we will upload all masks of quantitative experiments to the open-source platform.

\paragraph{Baseline.} We compared six adversarial proactive defense methods for diffusion models: PhotoGuard\cite{salman2023raising}, AdvDM \cite{liang2023adversarial}, MFA \cite{yu2024step}, Mist \cite{liang2023mist}, DiffusionGuard \cite{choi2024diffusionguard}, and DDD \cite{son2024disrupting}. To ensure a fair comparison, we will generate adversarial samples using the same adversarial perturbations budget and the same number of iterations. Furthermore, to align the experiment environment with real-world scenarios, we use skin masks and empty text prompts as training conditions to generate protected images. At the same time, we evaluate the protective performance of the protected images against inpainting manipulation guided by manual masks and malicious complete text prompts.

\paragraph{Technology details.} We use PGD\cite{madry2017towards} to optimize the adversarial samples, with the infinity norm of the perturbation constrained to 16/255 and a total of 800 optimization iterations for each adversarial sample. We use the Runway 1.5v version of the diffusion-based inpainter as our attack target. We also test the transferability of adversarial samples to the Stability AI 2.0v version of the diffusion-based inpainter\cite{rombach2022high}. We use NVIDIA 3090 GPUs to generate adversarial samples, and our method requires a maximum of 16GBs of GPU memory.
\subsection{Qualitative Results}
As shown in Figure \ref{fig3}, these are the manipulated images generated by the diffusion model based on three images sampled from CelebA-HQ guided by three different text prompts and six different manipulation region masks. 
The text prompts for images are: ``A photo of a construction worker'', ``A person in a gaming convention'' and ``A person walking in the street''. 
The manipulation reusults of the singular rows line are based on visible masks and the manipulation results of the even number rows are based on invisible masks. 
On the right of the dashed line in Figure \ref{fig3} are the tampering results of the diffusion model for images protected by different proactive defense methods.
Baseline models exhibit strong protection against malicious manipulation under known masks, but the defense effectiveness against manipulation guided by unknown conditions of baseline models is limited. However, Anti-Inpainting remains an outstanding protective performance against manipulation under unknown masks. 
Anti-Inpainting demonstrates superior protective effectiveness, causing the inpainting result under unknown conditions to become nearly devoid of recognizable content in most examples. 
In contrast, the inpainting result, which is based on images protected by baseline models under unknown conditions, is realistic and well-aligned with prompts. 
Furthermore, images protected by Anti-Inpainting effectively prevent the diffusion inpainting model from ‘understanding’ images, as illustrated in the final example where diffusion-based inpainters can not complement the body based on the protected face, and even draw an extra person on the background area.

