\subsection{Quantitative Results}
To test the effectiveness of our approach in enhancing the protection from inpainting manipulation, we conduct a quantitative comparison in  InpaintGuardBench and CelebA-HQ. 
To assess the effect of adversarial perturbation in diffusion-based inpainting models, we compute PSNR, SSIM \cite{wang2004image},  LPIPS\cite{zhang2018unreasonable} as the distance between the unprotected manipulation result and the protected manipulation result under the same random seed. 
To evaluate the visual quality of the manipulation result, we compute the image reward (IR) \cite{ xu2023imagereward} of the manipulation result. 
Lastly, we compute the arcface similarity (ARC sim)\cite{deng2019arcface} of the unprotected and protected manipulation results to assess the protective effectiveness for protected face identity information. 

Table \ref{table1} reports the strong protection of our approach. 
In PSNR, SSIM and LPIPS, our approach outperforms current mainstream methods. 
During the evaluation of the manipulation result's visual quality (IR), our method achieves the second-best performance in InpaintingGuardBench and superior performance in CelebA-HQ. 
Finally, our approach achieves best performance in disrupting the face identity information (ARC sim) of the manipulated images in InpaintingGuardBench and CelebA-HQ.
To simulate strong malicious tampering, we maliciously tamper the protected image under five random seeds in InpaintGuardBench and twenty random seeds in CelebA-HQ and take the best-tampered result among them as the final result. 
As shown in Table \ref{table2}, attributed to the Selection-based Distribution Deviation Optimization Strategy, our method achieves the best performance across all metrics on CelebA-HQ while attaining the best or second-best results on all evaluation metrics in InpaintGuardBench.
