\subsection{Ablation}
\paragraph{Extracted feature selection.} \label{feature selection}To demonstrate that using the outputs of each block in the diffusion model as part of the attack features is more efficient in enhancing the protective performance of adversarial perturbation, we set up the following ablation experiments. We divide the U-net of diffusion models into three parts: the downsampling blocks ~$\mathcal{D}$~, the upsampling blocks~$\mathcal{U}$~, and the middle layers~$\mathcal{M}$~. Then, we combine these three parts to obtain multiple control groups. By comparing them with a multi-level deep feature extractor, we find that our method based on the multi-level deep feature extractor achieves the best performance, as shown in Figure \ref{fig4}.

\paragraph{Impact of different optimization thresholds.} we update the adversarial perturbation ~$ delta $~ using different optimization thresholds~$ \tau$~ during optimization process. As the threshold ~$ \tau$~ varies, the performance of models changes synchronously for both known and unknown conditions. So, we set the size of the hyperparameter threshold ~$ \tau$~ based on the performance of adversarial perturbation under known conditions.
Figure \ref{fig4} shows that either an excessively large or small optimization threshold~$ \tau$~ will reduce the attack performance. Our method achieves the best protective performance only when the threshold~$ \tau$~ is moderate. Our explanation of the phenomenon is that the model may ignore some latent states worth optimizing when the threshold~$ \tau$~  is too tiny. Our method's optimization efficiency decreases due to inefficient optimization when the threshold~$ \tau$~  is too large.

\paragraph{Impact of augmentation intensities.} The dilation kernel of data augmentation is derived based on the augmentation intensity~$\gamma $~, so we test our method under different augmentation intensities, as shown in Figure \ref{fig4}. We found that as the data augmentation intensity~$\gamma $~ increases, the protective performance of the adversarial perturbation against manipulation guided by unknown conditions also continuously improves, which verifies the role of data augmentation in enhancing the protective performance of adversarial perturbation. Figure \ref{fig5} illustrates the distribution of adversarial perturbations under different data augmentation intensities. As the intensity of data augmentation increases, the coverage area of adversarial perturbations in the image gradually spreads, and its distribution becomes more uniform. This experiment illustrates the significant effect of data augmentation on the region distribution of adversarial perturbation.
\subsection{Transferability}
Transferability refers to the ability of adversarial examples generated based on known models to apply to other models. In this experiment, we tested the transferability of adversarial examples from Runway's v1.5 to StableAI's v2.0. It is worth noting that although these two models have the same architecture, their training protocols differ. As shown in Table \ref{table3}, our method shows approximate protective performance with the current best method in CelebA-HQ and the best protective effect in InpaintGuardBench.
\subsection{Robustness}
\begin{figure*}%[H]
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ robust1.pdf}
	\caption{The protective performance of baseline models and Anti-Inpainting through various purification methods.}
	\label{fig6}
\end{figure*}
We conduct additional experiments to demonstrate the robustness of Anti-Inpainting against various image purification techniques. Specifically, we evaluate the performance under three primary scenarios. 
In the first scenario, we apply JPEG compression with quality factors of 90, 75, and 50 to disrupt adversarial perturbation.
In the second scenario, Images undergo 50\% downsampling followed by upsampling to original dimensions using nearest-neighbor, bilinear, and bicubic interpolation methods.
The third scenario is bit reduction, in which we implement 4-bit to 1-bit quantization schemes to simulate information degradation in lossy storage environments.
These experiments are conducted using the diffusion-based inpainters in InpaintGuardBench. The quantitative results for PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS are presented in Figure \ref{fig6}. 
Our experimental results demonstrate considerable quantitative superiority of our proactive defense model over baseline approaches across both various JPEG quality factors and bit reductions, confirming the robustness of our adversarial perturbation against common image compression defenses. 
In addition, our experimental findings indicate that adversarial perturbations establish quantifiable outperformance relative to baseline approaches across diverse resizing interpolation methods, substantiating the robustness of our approach in circumventing defense mechanisms reliant on spatial transformation variations.
The robustness of our method stems from the perturbation distribution homogenization induced by multi-scale semantics-preserving data augmentation, which effectively protects perturbations against degradation caused by lossy operations.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper presents Anti-Inpainting, a novel proactive defense method designed to counter malicious diffusion-based inpainting manipulation under unknown operational conditions.
Specifically, our approach integrates three key innovations:
Initially, We deploy a multi-level deep feature extractor to capture intricate features, thereby significantly enhancing the protected images' protective efficacy against inpainting manipulations.
By incorporating multi-scale semantic-preserving data augmentation during optimization, we substantially improve the transferability of adversarial perturbation across diverse guidance conditions.
To mitigate ineffective updates, a selection-based distribution deviation optimization strategy is developed. This mechanism dynamically adjusts the adversarial noise distribution across multiple initial latent states.
%Extensive experiments demonstrate the protective performance under unknown conditions and robustness against various purification methods of Anti-Inpainting.
Our approach is supported by extensive experiments results, and emerges as a powerful proactive defense method to protect images from malicious inpainting manipulation under unknow conditions.

